//
// File: models/api/management.tsp
//
// Written by hand (#1390). THE MANAGEMENT API OPENAPI DOCUMENT'S SOURCE:
// `models/tools/generate.mjs` compiles it during the image build, the service
// serves the result and holds every answer to it.
//
// The reasoning below sat beside the code this replaced.
//
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The OpenAPI document for the management API, and the schemas it is written
// in.
//
// It is BUILT FROM THE ROUTE TABLE in admin_api.ts rather than kept beside it
// as a hand-written YAML file, and that is the whole point of this module. A
// spec file next to the code it describes is a spec file that is wrong within a
// month: somebody adds an action to the console, adds it to the API, and does
// not touch the document — and the only thing that then reports the drift is a
// person reading both. Here the operation and its description are ONE OBJECT,
// so an operation that exists is documented by construction and one that is
// documented exists.
//
// The counterpart rule is in admin_api.ts: an /admin control gets an /admin-api
// operation in the same commit. That one cannot be enforced by construction —
// nothing can see a form appear on a page — so it is asserted by this
// repository's own tests/protocol/admin_api.js (a `local: true` job) instead,
// which walks the console's own NAV and action lists and fails on a page or an
// action with no operation.
//
// OpenAPI 3.1.0 rather than 3.0.3: this document uses JSON Schema `examples`,
// `const` and nullable-by-union, all of which 3.0 spells differently or not at
// all. Every tool this repository would plausibly meet reads 3.1.
//
// This module registers no route and requires nothing of this service's but the
// LOGGER and `admin-core/protocol_endpoints.ts`, a table that holds no state —
// it is a pure function over a table — so its position in the require
// order does not matter, in the sense rule 3 gives for dpop.js, which reaches
// for helpers.js on exactly the same terms. What it must not grow is a require
// of anything that holds state: a document built from what the service happens
// to be doing is a document that describes one moment.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TYPESCRIPT, AS A CLASS (#50, 2026-09-16) — `common/realm_chooser.ts`'s
// shape: `AdminApiSpec` takes the modules it uses through its constructor
// (`AdminApiSpecDeps`). Since R2 the composition root
// (`common/protocol_stack.ts`) builds the instance, and the module's old
// names are FACADES that forward to it, for the JavaScript callers; a process
// without the root builds a default at load. `AdminApiSpec` is exported
// beside them for the root.
//
// **`openObject()` AND `pagingObject()` ARE STATIC, AND BOUND AT MODULE SCOPE
// under their old names**, because the schema tables below call them at
// load — some three hundred call sites that read exactly as they did. Since
// R2 nothing at load may use the instance (the root installs it later, and a
// facade called now would build a default the root's install then refuses),
// and the two need nothing an instance holds: they are pure shapes over
// `PAGING_PROPERTIES`, logging to the service logger. The tables themselves
// stay module-level constants, so `SCHEMAS` is the same value at load that
// `mgmt-api/admin_api.ts` has always read.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The reply shape shared by every POST here: the console's own action result,
// unchanged. `ok` is the only member that is always present; the rest depends
// on what was asked, which is why this schema is open rather than closed.
// Which console pages list their realm's endpoints, so a GET mirroring one
// can say that its reply carries them (2026-09-13). A library that requires
// nothing route-registering; see its header.
// One row of the issued list: a JWT, a SAML assertion or a Kerberos ticket. The
// signed artifact is NEVER in it — see the note on the console's tokens page —
// so what a caller gets is the facts about it and the jti to act on.
// The paging members every list here carries. Written once and mixed into the
// three list schemas, because a caller walking one of them has to be able to
// walk the others the same way.
// What `AdminApiSpec` needs from the rest of the service: the modules this file
// used to reach for itself, passed in so that the composition root can build
// one and a test can build one with stubs.
// What the composition root passes, from the real modules.
// The same members as an OBJECT, for a reply that carries more than one list
// and therefore cannot put them at the top level. Built from
// PAGING_PROPERTIES above rather than written out again, because two
// hand-kept copies of five member names is one copy that will eventually be
// missing `lastRow`.
//
// `total` is here and not up there for a reason worth stating: at the top
// level the number is called `matched`, which is the count AFTER a filter. A
// drill-down's lists have no filter, so the honest name for the number is the
// plain one.
// Static since R2: the tables call it at load (see the header).
// Static since R2: the tables call it at load (see the header).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PROSE AT THE TOP OF THE DOCUMENT, AND IT IS A FUNCTION OF THE GATE
// SINCE 2026-09-10 BECAUSE IT USED TO BE A FLAT CONTRADICTION OF IT.
//
// It is long on purpose: the first thing anybody pointing a tool at this
// needs to know is whether it is protected and that four of its operations
// change what the PROTOCOL endpoints do.
//
// **IT SAID "Nothing here is protected" FOR A DAY AFTER IT STOPPED BEING
// TRUE.** `/admin-api` began requiring an OAuth 2.0 access token on
// 2026-09-09; the startup banner was taught to read `adminApi.authRequired`
// and this document was not, so the one artifact a machine reads went on
// stating the opposite of what every one of its 238 operations would do. That
// is worse than a stale comment: `security: []` is OpenAPI's way of saying a
// credential is not needed, so a generated client sent none and was refused
// everywhere, and the sentence a person read told them the refusal was a bug.
//
// Both states are still real — `adminApi.authRequired` is the off switch and
// it restores the open API exactly — so the paragraph is written twice rather
// than hedged once. The OFF text is the original, verbatim, because it is the
// argument for the switch.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ===========================================================================
// THE SECURITY HALF OF THE DOCUMENT.
//
// **THE SCOPE A GIVEN OPERATION NEEDS IS DECIDED BY ITS METHOD, AND THE
// AUTHORITY IS THE GATE RATHER THAN THIS FILE.** `admin_api.ts`'s
// `app.use(BASE, ...)` middleware reads `req.method === 'GET' ? 'admin:read'
// : 'admin:write'`, and `scopeForMethod()` below is that one line written a
// second time — which is a duplication worth naming, because a document that
// disagrees with the gate is exactly the failure this whole change is about.
// `tests/admin_api_document_security.js` compares the two over every
// operation in the table, so the copy cannot drift silently.
//
// TWO SCHEMES, and they describe the same credential from the two ends a
// reader arrives from:
//
//   * `oauth2` (client credentials) is what the API actually wants and where
//     it comes from — the token endpoint, the two scopes, the client. It is
//     the accurate description, and it is the one a tool can act on.
//   * `bearerAuth` (http/bearer) is for the reader who already HAS a token —
//     out of a launcher's environment, out of the console's explorer, out of
//     a shell — and wants to paste it into a tool's Authorize box. Nothing
//     but a header, which is all such a reader needs.
//
// **`resource` HAS NO FIELD IN OPENAPI AND IT IS THE PARAMETER MOST LIKELY TO
// BE MISSED**, so it is in the scheme's own description rather than left to
// the prose at the top: a token minted without it carries the wrong `aud` and
// is refused by a gate that has just told the caller the credential was good
// enough to parse. Being explicit here is the difference between a
// five-second fix and reading a middleware.
// ===========================================================================
//
// **ONE OPERATION IS THE EXCEPTION, AND THE GATE SAYS SO THE SAME WAY**
// (#164 phase 3): `POST /admin-api/device-compliance`, the MDM feed, takes
// `device:compliance` and not `admin:write` — `admin_api.ts`'s
// `isDeviceComplianceFeed()` — so a posture feed holds nothing else.
// THE CONSOLE'S OWN OPERATIONS (#454), as the gate's `consoleOp` reads
// them: everything under `/admin-api/console` takes `admin:console`.
// A DIRECTORY → POLICIES WRITE (#645): every action of
// `/admin-api/policies/{action}` is a policy kind's save, reset or act,
// which the gate asks `admin:security` for
// (`common/security_operations.ts`). The other security changes depend
// on the body — which attribute, whose account — so their operations
// say `admin:write` and the scheme's description says the rest.
// #645.
// What one operation requires. Two alternatives rather than one, because the
// schemes above are two ways to present the same credential and OpenAPI reads
// a LIST of requirement objects as "any one of these will do". The bearer
// entry carries an empty array because scopes are meaningless outside oauth2
// — naming them there would be a document that validators reject.
// One operation, as OpenAPI wants it. `entry` is a row of admin_api.ts's
// route table and `action` is one of its actions, or null for a plain route.
// THE ONE MEMBER NO VIEW ADDS: `mgmt-api/admin_api.ts`'s `sendJson()` puts
// it on the reply of a GET mirroring exactly one Protocols page, so it is
// said here from the same test rather than in forty descriptions.
// A BODY THAT IS A FILE (#215, `POST /admin-api/risk/upload`): the bytes
// themselves under each type the operation takes, never JSON.
// EVERY POST DOCUMENTS ITS 400, not only the action resources. A refusal is
// the reply half a caller has to handle, and it is not a property of being
// an ACTION — a plain POST that takes a body can refuse it just as an
// action can refuse its name. This was found by the parity test rather than
// reasoned about: `POST /admin-api/keys/export` is the first non-action
// POST this API has had, and it went in documenting a 200 alone.
// The statuses an operation answers beyond 200 and 400 (#215's upload:
// 202 stored and loading, 413, 415, 507), each an ActionResult.
// THE ANSWER'S MODEL (#1353): `response_models.ts`, per operation and
// status, with the member `sendJson()` adds for a mirrored Protocols page.
// The document. `routes` is admin_api.ts's table; an entry carrying `actions`
// becomes one operation per action, at the concrete URL each of them has —
// which is a real address even though express serves all of them from one
// `:action` pattern.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// **THE GATE'S STATE ARRIVES AS AN OPTION AND DEFAULTS TO ON.**
//
// It is not read from `config` here, and the header of this file is why:
// this module must not require anything that holds state, or the document
// becomes a description of one moment rather than of the API. `baseUrl` and
// `version` already arrive the same way — from a caller that has the
// request in its hand — and `adminApi.authRequired` is one more such fact.
// `admin_api.ts`'s `specOptions()` is the one place all three are gathered,
// so a fourth caller cannot assemble a different answer.
//
// **THE DEFAULT IS THE SETTING'S OWN DEFAULT, AND THE DIRECTION MATTERS.**
// A caller that forgets to pass it makes a document that OVER-states the
// requirement, which costs a client one unnecessary token; the other
// default would reproduce the exact bug this option was added for, where a
// client is told no credential is needed by an API that refuses it on every
// operation.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CONSOLE'S DOCUMENT (#454), `GET /admin-api/console/openapi.json`:
// the same builder over the console's rows, under its own title and with
// a first paragraph saying what those operations are.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE DOCUMENT-WIDE DEFAULT, AND EVERY OPERATION STATES ITS OWN AS WELL.
//
// Redundant on purpose. A tool's Authorize box reads THIS one — so it has
// to name both scopes, which is what the token a reader will actually
// hold carries — while the per-operation entries say the narrower truth,
// that a GET needs only `admin:read`. Neither alone is both actionable
// and accurate.
//
// **AN EMPTY ARRAY IS NOT AN OMISSION, IT IS THE OPPOSITE CLAIM**, and
// that is why it may only appear when the gate is off: OpenAPI reads
// `security: []` as "no credential is needed", which is exactly what
// `adminApi.authRequired: false` means and exactly what this document
// wrongly said while the gate was on.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE HELPERS THE TABLES BELOW CALL at load, under their old names so that
// every call site in them reads as it did. Static, so no instance is used.
// One rule of an ordered setting's Select all (#1130), as
// `config.describe()` reports it.
// One row of config.js's table, as this API reports it. Written out rather than
// left open because every property here is one a generator would want a name
// for, and because `source` is the answer to the question the whole resource
// exists for: a value alone does not say whether somebody set it, the container
// set it, the file set it, or nobody did.
// #86: a `csv` row whose entries come from a closed set says which, so a
// client can draw the choices rather than learn them from a refusal.
// 2026-10-01: a closed list whose ORDER is a preference
// (`webauthn.algorithms`), and a sentence per value.
// #1130: what an ordered setting's Select all ticks, where another
// setting narrows it (`webauthn.algorithms`: RS1 only while
// webauthn.insecureAlgorithms is on, ML-DSA alone while
// webauthn.pqcOnly is).
// Present on an `int` setting whose row narrows it, and absent everywhere
// else — the same way `enumValues` is present on an enum and nowhere else.
// They are DOCUMENTED rather than left implicit because a client rendering
// an input for a setting has no other way to learn what will be refused,
// and the token lifetimes are the settings somebody actually types a
// number into.
// `legacyEnv` is not documented while no setting carries one (#523
// retired the last, STS_ISSUER): `describe()` still sends it for a row
// that has one, and the next such row puts the property back here.
// THE SETTINGS BLOCK EVERY PAGE THAT OWNS SETTINGS CARRIES, written once for
// the same reason CONFIG_SETTING above is: since 2026-08-27 twenty-one console
// pages draw their own settings, and their `settings` member is this shape on
// every one of them. A caller reads it once.
// WHAT THE BLOCK'S PROSE STATES ABOUT THE PROCESS (#446): a console
// drawn in a browser has no process to ask.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PROPERTIES BOTH CLAIM-SET REPLIES CARRY.
//
// GET /admin-api/claims answers for the two JWT sets and GET
// /admin-api/saml-attributes for the two SAML ones — two console pages onto
// one store since 2026-08-24 — and everything except ONE property each is the
// same shape answered twice. So the shape is written once and each schema adds
// what is genuinely its own: `reservedJwtClaims` for the tokens, because that
// list is enforced for `kind === 'jwt'` alone, and `defaultSaml11Namespace`
// for the assertions, because nothing else has one.
//
// Copied instead, this would be the pair of schemas that disagree about
// `attributeCatalogue` within a month — and this repository's
// tests/protocol/admin_api.js checks each documented property against a LIVE
// reply, so
// the disagreement would surface as a test failure naming a property rather
// than as anything pointing here.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `protocolEndpoints` on a GET mirroring a Protocols page: `sendJson()` adds it
// outside the view every model describes (`admin-core/protocol_endpoints.ts`).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SIGN-OUT INVENTORY.
//
// One shape for both answers `GET /admin-api/logout` gives — the family list
// when no `user` was named, and one identity's live state when one was —
// because the two differ by which members are present rather than in kind,
// which is the same arrangement UserList/UserDetail has one screen down.
//
// `terminable: false` rows are the members most likely to be mistaken for a
// defect, so the description says what they are where a generated client's
// documentation will show it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// EVERY ARRAY HERE IS ONE PAGE, not the whole list — the same thing
// `users` is on the list beside it — and every one is answered by the
// `*Paging` object under it. Five lists on one reply is why they are
// paged separately: `sessionsPage`, `endedPage`, `sessionlessPage` and
// `artifactsPage` each move one of them, and `per` is shared.
// THE SAME IDEA FOR A PERSON, and the same argument for publishing it: what
// `POST /admin-api/users/create` accepts in `attributes` is a CLOSED
// catalogue — the attributes vc_claims.js says a person in this directory
// has — and a name that is not on it is refused rather than dropped. A
// caller that reads this cannot construct a create the service will refuse.
//
// It is the same list the console's /admin/users/new form is drawn from, and
// that is the point rather than a coincidence: one catalogue read through two
// doors, so a field on the form and a field this document offers cannot come
// apart.
// WHAT A CREATE MAY SAY, answered by the service rather than described in
// this document. The two vocabularies below are the closed lists
// createApplication() validates against, so a caller that reads this cannot
// construct a create the service will refuse — the property
// editableAttributes() gives the console's two selects, reached over HTTP.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FIVE DIRECTORY PAGES, 2026-09-01.
//
// `/admin/ldap/directory`, `/admin/ldap/applications`,
// `/admin/ldap/federations`, `/admin/ldap/spiffe` and
// `/admin/ldap/service` were HTML pages outside the console until that day
// and are console pages now; rule 7 gave each of them an operation, and
// these are what those operations answer with.
//
// EVERY ONE OF THEM IS DELIBERATELY SHALLOW, and it is worth saying why
// rather than leaving it to look like an omission. What these resources
// return is DIRECTORY ENTRIES, and this directory is SCHEMALESS on purpose
// — an entry may carry whatever attributes a client sent it. So an
// `attributes` member written out property by property would be a document
// making a promise the store does not keep, and the first `ldapadd` of an
// unknown attribute would make it false. The names ARE published, and in
// the one place that can keep them right: each of these replies carries the
// container's own `schema`, read out of the module that owns it. That is
// the same argument the pages themselves make for printing a schema at all.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// #616: what a windowed process could and could not ask of the store.
// THE THREE ADDED ON 2026-09-05. Each of these containers' owning modules
// published a SCHEMA whose comment said it was drawn under `/admin/ldap/*`,
// and for three of them no page had ever been written — the export was dead
// in `common/roles.js`, `xacml/xacml_store.ts` and
// `xacml/xacml_pep_registry.ts`. The pages exist now, so rule 7 owes each of
// them an operation here.
// Beside `group` rather than inside it, because they describe THIS REPLY
// and not the directory entry — whose own memberCount, presentCount and
// danglingCount are untouched next to them and stay counts of the whole
// list.
// The shape every page that OWNS SETTINGS answers with, and one schema
// rather than one per page: the protocol settings pages (eight when this
// was written, thirteen now) differ in prose and in which group they draw,
// and a schema per page would have been that many copies of this. It is
// also what the `settings` member of the SAML, SCIM, SPIFFE, federation and
// directory pages carries, so a caller learns one shape and reads it
// everywhere.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// XACML. Three schemas, because the three GETs answer three different
// questions and one loose object covering all of them would document none.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TWO POLICIES THAT DECIDE THIS SERVICE'S OWN BOUNDARIES AND ARE NOT IN
// THE REPOSITORY (2026-09-06).
//
// `role-issuance` gates the nine issuance sites and `access-control` gates
// surfaces `common/access_gate.ts` guards. Both are BUILT IN — the
// template is called at decision time rather than seeded into `ou=policies`,
// because that container is per trust realm — so neither appears in
// `policies` above and neither is offered by the editor's chooser.
//
// It is DESCRIBED here rather than left to `additionalProperties: true`
// because the omission is the whole defect this member was added for: a
// caller reading `policies` and stopping would conclude that whatever is
// root there is what this service enforces, and on an ordinary service that
// is a seeded example which enforces nothing.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// #564: the application that owns the policy, and when that
// application was deleted; null for an ordinary policy.
// #564, P5: the applications' own policies, grouped by owner.
// WHAT AUTHORIZATION IS DOING, as opposed to what it is configured to do.
// Every other XACML schema here describes the repository; this one describes
// TRAFFIC, and the two things it is careful about are the two things a
// caller would otherwise get wrong.
// WHAT THE PROVISIONING SURFACE IS DOING, as opposed to what it is. `Scim`
// above describes the SURFACE and carries the headline counts; this one is
// the TRAFFIC, and the two are views over ONE set of counters rather than two
// tallies. Three things in here are easy to get wrong from the outside and
// each is written out rather than left open for that reason.
// WHAT THIS CONSOLE HAS BEEN TOLD. It is the receiving half of Shared
// Signals and is deliberately not folded into `Ssf`, which is the
// TRANSMITTER's view — the streams, the subjects, the queues and what went
// out on each. A reply that carried both would answer "what has been said"
// and "what has been heard" with one document, and the whole value of this
// resource is that it goes empty when delivery is broken while the other
// one does not.
// WHAT THE TRANSMITTER COULD NOT DELIVER, COUNTED (2026-09-14). A resource
// of its own rather than a member of `Ssf` for the reason `Signals` is one:
// `Ssf` is each stream's configuration with its first letters, and this is
// counts over every stream at once plus the letters searched and paged —
// the view somebody takes during an incident rather than while setting a
// stream up.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DELEGATION. Written out rather than left open for the reason AuditEvent is:
// a caller filtering, alerting on or DRAWING this list needs a name for every
// field, and the picture is the point of it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every value a recording site writes (2026-09-19): the list said
// four while rows carried nine, a source search of `channel: '`
// being how the rest were found. A new value needs a row here.
// THE CONSOLE'S DOCUMENT'S FIRST PARAGRAPH (#454).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE GATE, WHEN IT IS ON — which is the default.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AND WHEN IT IS OFF (`adminApi.authRequired: false`). The original text.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The paragraphs that are true whichever way the switch is set.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE INSTANCE, BUILT BY THE COMPOSITION ROOT (#50, R2). This module builds no
// instance of its own: `common/protocol_stack.ts` builds one and calls
// `installInstance()`. The exports below are FACADES that forward to that
// instance, for the JavaScript that still calls this module through
// `require()`; a process that never runs the root gets a default instance,
// built from `defaultDeps()` when the module loads (see
// `common/instance_slot.ts`).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Standalone, build the default now, as loading this module always did.
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE MANAGEMENT API ANSWERS, PER OPERATION AND STATUS (#1353).
//
// `admin_api_spec.ts` is generated from `admin_api.ts`'s table, and a row of that
// table says what a request may carry. What each answer carries is here, in one
// file per family (`response_models_*.ts`), keyed by operationId: nine hundred
// models inside a 29,000-line table would make that table's rows unreadable and
// every other change to it a conflict. A new operation without a model fails
// `tests/openapi_response_models.js`, so the split cannot hide one.
//
// **EVERY OPERATION'S OTHER STATUSES ARE `default`**, `ERROR` below: a gate's
// refusal arrives before any handler and is the same for every operation.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Merged once: `apply()` asks per operation, and there are eight hundred.
// A refusal, whichever door answered it: the gate's RFC 6750 pair, or an
// action's `{ ok: false, errors }`.
// `why` is the XACML actions' sentence beside `errors`, and `status` what the
// enrollment families' core says it refused with.
// Each family's named schemas, for `admin_api_spec.ts`'s `SCHEMAS`.
// The operations a family has modelled, for the test that all of them are.
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESPONSE MODELS (#1353): Applications, OAuth 2.0 / OIDC, Tokens,
// Authorization servers, OpenID4VCI, OpenID4VP, Verifier request, Credential
// claims, Credential status, Token lifetimes.
//
// `operations` is keyed by operationId, each value naming the statuses that
// have a model of their own; `components` replaces a named schema of
// `admin_api_spec.ts`. `common/response_model_kit.ts` says how a model is
// written and `mgmt-api/response_models.ts` how these are applied.
//
// **EVERY MODEL HERE IS THE UNION OF WHAT THE BRANCHES BEHIND ITS OPERATION CAN
// SEND, READ FROM THE CODE**, because an answer that fails its model is not
// sent. Traffic is a lower bound and was used only to check the reading.
//
// **A VALUE THAT COMES OUT OF A TOKEN'S CLAIMS IS NOT THIS SERVICE'S TO TYPE.**
// `recordJwt()` copies `client_id || azp || aud` and an `aud` may be an array
// (RFC 7519 section 4.1.3), and a configured custom claim may carry a number or
// a list under a name the record reads. Those members are `CLAIM_VALUE`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A value of one of a person's or a token's claims: never an object here,
// because every reader of these records treats it as a name.
// What a page's JSON says about its paging, `pagingJson()`.
// The same members flattened into an answer beside its rows.
// `protocolEndpoints`: `sendJson()` adds it to a GET mirroring a Protocols page
// outside the view (`admin_api_spec.ts` says it too, for the models it can
// read).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ONE SETTING, `common/config.js`'s `describe()`. A setting's value is what its
// row's type parses to: bool, int, string, or a `csv`'s list. `default` is the
// row's `dflt`, which a function may compute and which may be null.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `configSettingsJson()`: what every page that owns settings answers.
// `protocolSettingsJson()`, for the three protocol settings pages in this
// family. None of those three rows has a `status` block. A model of their own
// rather than `PageSettings`, whose other pages carry other status blocks.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT WAS ISSUED: `issuedList()` in `common/admin_stats.js`.
//
// THE MEMBERS OF A ROW DEPEND ON ITS FAMILY: a token row is `recordJwt()`'s and
// an artifact's is `recordArtifact()`'s, and they share few. `IssuedRecord` is
// read by `tests/protocol/admin_api.js`, which holds that every name in
// `properties` is on a live row of the list (a token row, the kind that job has
// just minted). So `properties` keeps what every token row carries, and what
// only some rows carry is `patternProperties`, one exact name each: as closed
// to `additionalProperties: false` as a declared member, and honest about being
// conditional.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// On every token row, and on no artifact's unless it says so below.
// `recordArtifact()`, and each of its four families' own.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A CREDENTIAL'S LINEAGE: `credentialLineageModel()`, over the delegation
// store's acts (`common/delegation.js`) and `common/credential_graph.ts`, whose
// graph is `delegation.graph()`'s with its own nodes and edges added.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `userGraph.flowRow()` or `artifactFlowRow()`: their rows and placeholders.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TEN TOKEN ACTIONS, `tokenAction()`. One action's `revoked` is a count;
// the
// `set` member of the set actions is the key that was asked for.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// USED ASSERTIONS, `usedAssertionsView()` over `common/used_assertions.js`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CREDENTIAL STATUS, `vcStatus.summary()` and `statusAction()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TOKEN LIFETIMES.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT A CREDENTIAL CARRIES: `vcJson()` and `vcAction()`.
//
// A subject's claims are the catalogue's (`VC_ATTRIBUTES`), one level of
// nesting
// at most (`address`, `place_of_birth`), and each value a directory value or a
// generated one: never deeper.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `populateDirectory()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE VERIFIER ASKS FOR: `vpConfigJson()`, `vpConfigAction()`, and what
// `vc_verifier.ts` keeps of each presentation.
//
// A DCQL query here is always one this service built (`dcqlQuery()`,
// `signInDcqlQuery()`): no caller supplies one. `type_values` holds a list per
// entry for a JWT VC, whose identifier is itself a list.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `record.verdict`: a declined answer, a malformed one, a verified one, or a
// self-issued ID Token's, at the four places `vc_verifier.ts` writes it.
// The open list, or one presentation by its `state`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OPENID4VCI: what the credential issuer saw. `event_description` is the
// wallet's own word, kept as it arrived.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AUTHORIZATION SERVERS, `oauth-oidc/authorization_servers.ts`.
//
// `overrides`, a drift row's `published` and `actual`, and `capabilities` are
// free-form for the reason that module's catalogue is "help, not schema": any
// member name may be set to any JSON value (`setMember()` parses what it is
// given), so that a document a client did not expect can be published.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The list, or one profile: one closed object, as `APPLICATION_LIST` says
// below.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE OAUTH 2.0 / OIDC MONITOR, `oauth2_monitor_console.ts` over `par.ts`.
//
// A pushed request's `parameters` are what the client pushed, a request
// object's
// claims among them: its names and values are the client's.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GRANT MANAGEMENT, `grant_management.ts`. A row is `decorate()`d, or is
// `list()`'s
// bare row where the grant went between the two reads. `authorization_details`
// is what the client asked for (RFC 9396): each type's fields are its own.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CLAIMS PROVIDERS, `claims_providers.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OPENID PROVIDER COMMANDS, `provider_commands.ts` over `outbound_delivery.ts`.
// `roles` and `last_access` are what a relying party said of itself, each held
// to its shape where it arrives (`rolesFrom()`, `lastAccessFrom()`).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 2.2's role object, which `ProviderCommands.rolesFrom()` holds a
// relying party's answer to.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ONE APPLICATION: `view()` in `common/applications.js`. An entry's attribute
// names are data, the directory being schemaless on purpose, and a value is one
// string or a list, or a typed value a write left there.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each list row of `GET /applications` is masked, with its secret's expiry.
// `applicationFields()` through `typedField()`. An override carries the setting
// it overrides, `described`, and the form's `inSimple` is the new-form page's.
// `applicationsListJson()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ONE APPLICATION'S PAGE, `applicationDetailJson()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `pagedRows()`'s paging, which `pagingJson()` narrows: this one has `offset`.
// `certificateSummary()`, which answers `{ unreadable }` for what will not
// parse.
// `KEY_PAIR_ATTRIBUTES` in `common/applications.js`.
// The registered half: a JWKS's keys for `jwt`, certificates for `saml`.
// A row of `tlsClientCertificates.listFor()`, narrowed by its two callers.
// The revoked row itself, which `revoke()` answers as `listFor()` built it.
// `applicationStoredCertificatesOf()`, whose refusal has `errors`.
// `applicationCredentialsState().json`.
// The same state as the page has it: `applicationPageData()` takes `json` and
// two
// secrets off, and keeps the rest.
// A software statement's `metadata` is RFC 7591 client metadata, the client's.
// A claim row, `checkClaimEntries()`, as a realm's set and an application's
// hold
// it; `effective` adds where each came from.
// `applicationEnrollmentState()`. A certificate record is what the core stored,
// less its private key: `publicRecord()`. `device` and `attestation` are a
// device's; `revoked` appears once it is.
// `appPermissions.register()`.
// One audit row, `common/audit.js`'s `record()`. `detailOf()` keeps a number, a
// boolean or a trimmed string under any name its caller gave.
// An access type, `authorizationDetailsTypeOf()` (#432, #564). `schema`,
// `limits`
// and `source` are the JSON an operator wrote; a compiled `validate` is a
// function, which JSON leaves out, so only its null is ever sent.
// `xacml/fga_policies.ts`'s `state()`, with the page's three lists of choices.
// What `krb5_trusts.ts`'s `applicationAcross()` draws for an application.
// `applicationObservedState()`.
// `applicationPageData()`: what the console's page is drawn from. The name
// `page`
// cannot collide with an attribute, and is a number on the list reply.
// `applicationDetailJson()`, `{ found: false, identifier }` for one nobody
// holds,
// and an owner's narrowing of it (#607), whose `owners` and `maxOwners` are the
// page's lifted out. So every member is optional.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE APPLICATION ACTIONS, `applicationsAction()`. `application` is the view
// after
// a write, or the identifier where the action answers only its name. `changed`
// is
// a boolean, and the list of attributes on `update-fields`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `tlsClientCertificates.bundle()`. Each file travels as `text` or as `base64`.
// What `sp_metadata.ts`'s `consume()` says of a document it read, with its
// refusal.
// `protected_resource_metadata.ts`'s `analyse()` or a refusal. The document is
// the
// resource's own (RFC 9728), and a refusal may name a status or the members
// read.
// A list row's `$ref` keeps the operation's name; `getApplications` is a list
// or
// one application, whose `page` is a number on one and an object on the other.
//
// ONE CLOSED OBJECT, NOT `oneOf`: a validator's refusal of a union names every
// branch's members, and STS-API-0139 is read by a person. Nothing is opened by
// it, since each member is still declared and typed.
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESPONSE MODELS (#1353): the console-only operations (`kind: console`,
// `/admin-api/console/*`).
//
// `operations` is keyed by operationId, each value naming the statuses that
// have a model of their own; `components` replaces a named schema of
// `admin_api_spec.ts`. `common/response_model_kit.ts` says how a model is
// written and `mgmt-api/response_models.ts` how these are applied.
//
// **THE DRAWINGS SHARE ONE GRAPH.** A node or an edge here came from one of
// three builders: `common/delegation.js`'s `graph()`, `common/app_permissions`'
// `graph()` (which reuses the shape and adds what a grant knows) and
// `common/user_graph.ts`'s `graphFor()` (which adds what an issuance knows). A
// page reads them through one renderer, so they are one model, closed, with
// every member any of the three can write and none of them required.
//
// **A DRAWING'S `format=svg` IS A SECOND MEDIA TYPE ON THE SAME 200**, so those
// five operations are `Kit.media('image/svg+xml')` with the JSON model under
// `schema`.
//
// **EVERY TOP LEVEL HERE IS A `closed()` OBJECT, NEVER A UNION.**
// `ResponseModels.apply()` adds `protocolEndpoints` to a model's `properties`
// for a GET mirroring a Protocols page, and a union has none to add it to.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A number where the source does not promise a whole one: a time read back out
// of a store, a count summed from a register row.
// `delegation.js`'s `party()`.
// `delegation.js`'s `credential()`, one kind's worth on an act.
// `recordUnguarded()`, and what another process's replicated row carries. An
// act written before a member existed lacks it, so nothing here is required.
// `chainList()`, one row per relationship.
// `foldCredentials()`'s row: every credential of one kind on one line.
// The grants' picture (`app_permissions.ts`).
// The person's picture (`user_graph.ts`, `normaliseNode()`).
// One family of sign-ins on the person's `signed-in` line.
// A configured pair beside the acts (#186).
// A grant's line. `app_permissions.ts` seeds the two folds and nothing
// empties them, so they are always `{}` there.
// The person's picture.
// `user_graph.ts`'s `credentialsFor()`.
// `app_permissions.ts`.
// `user_graph.ts`.
// `delegationNodeLook()`.
// `delegationFacts()`: the strings in an answer that name somebody. The keys
// are those strings, whatever the answer held.
// `userGraph`'s `rowFromStats()` and `foldDelegation()`.
// `delegationChooser()`.
// A setting's value is what its row's type says: a scalar, or a `csv` row's
// list. `dflt` may be a function's answer, of the same kinds.
// `common/config.js`'s `describe()`.
// `admin.ts`'s `configSettingsJson()`, or null with no filler installed.
// `Negotiated` in `common/i18n.ts`.
// `PageLocale.consoleData()`, or `consoleLanguage()` with `catalogs=files`.
// The sidebar's row: a page, or a group of them that `isNavGroup()` recurses
// into. SECTIONS nests no deeper than a group inside a section, and a group
// inside a group is written out once more rather than left unstated.
// `AdminApi.consoleGateOf()`.
// `realms.js`'s `retiringState()`.
// `shellRuntimeFacts()`: all of these, the mode alone, or none.
// The graph's fields, spread at the top of the map's answer by
// `Object.assign({}, graph, ...)`.
// `user_graph.ts`'s FLOWS, or `flowRow()` naming one it has no row for.
// `permissionGroupsView()`'s two branches and what the drawing adds to either.
// FEDERATION_GRAPH's `applicationRows()`.
// `describe()` and what `graph()` adds to each row.
// An attribute's `editable`: the schema row's word, or false for one that is
// not (`declarationAttributes()` copies the row as it stands).
// `applications.js`'s `applicationFields()` through `typedField()`.
// `pki_authoring.ts`'s `view()`. What the form holds is under `draft`.
//
// A draft is a map because its keys are the form's own field list, which this
// same answer publishes as `fields` (115 names, `FIELDS` in that file, three of
// them written by name beside it and a family of `pki_ku_*` by a loop over
// x509's bits): `apply-profile` rewrites them and a client echoes them back, so
// the service's vocabulary here is data the document reads from the service.
// The pane's four helpers: the form and how to draw it, or `refuse()`'s pair
// and a schema's or the catch's `{ ok, errors }`.
// `admin_api_spec.ts`'s `buildSpec()`: closed at the top, and free below it.
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESPONSE MODELS (#1353): Kerberos, LDAP, Directory, TLS, WS-Trust,
// WS-Federation, SCIM, SAML 2.0, SAML 1.1, SAML assertions, Custom SAML
// attributes, Custom claims, UserInfo claims.
//
// `operations` is keyed by operationId, each value naming the statuses that
// have a model of their own; `components` replaces a named schema of
// `admin_api_spec.ts`. `common/response_model_kit.ts` says how a model is
// written and `mgmt-api/response_models.ts` how these are applied.
//
// **AN ANSWER THAT FAILS ITS MODEL IS NOT SENT**, so each model below is the
// union of every member any branch of its builder can send, read from the
// builder; recorded traffic only ever checked it. Three things this family
// has that are easy to get wrong:
//
//   * **A DIRECTORY ENTRY'S ATTRIBUTE NAMES ARE DATA** (`mapOf`, `ATTRIBUTES`),
//     because this directory is schemaless on purpose: an `ldapadd` can write
//     any name. The record a registry derives from an entry is the opposite,
//     its members are this service's own, and it is `closed`.
//   * **A GET MIRRORING A PROTOCOLS PAGE GETS `protocolEndpoints` ADDED**, by
//     `ResponseModels.apply()`, and only to a model with top-level
//     `properties`. So those models are one closed object and never a `oneOf`,
//     and a read with two shapes (the directory pages' "no directory is
//     loaded", a list and its drill-down) is the same object with both sets of
//     members optional.
//   * **`FEDERATION_RELATIONSHIP` REPEATS `federation/federation.js`'s SCHEMA
//     BY NAME**: `ldapFederationsView()` copies a record whose members are that
//     table's attributes, so a new attribute there owes a line here or
//     `GET /admin-api/ldap/federations` stops answering once a relationship
//     exists.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A moment some module keeps in milliseconds and another as text; null until
// it happened.
// `protocolEndpoints` on a GET mirroring a Protocols page. `apply()` adds it to
// a model whose page has endpoints; it is written out here as well on the ones
// recorded carrying it, so that the model read alone is the whole answer.
// One attribute of a directory entry, or one recorded field of a registry:
// its name is data. A value is a list as the entry holds it, or one string
// where a registry keeps a single-valued field, and `withoutSecrets()` hands
// a null it was given straight back.
// `AdminViews.pagingJson()`.
// What `directoryPageJson()` answers in a process with no directory loaded.
// The `SCHEMA` every directory container publishes. One model for all of them:
// the columns differ per module (applications.js has the most, roles.js two)
// and all of them are optional, and `editable` is a boolean on some rows and
// 'single' or 'multi' on others.
// `pagedRows()` at the top of a directory page.
// `entryObject()`: a directory entry as the registries read it.
// The bounded look inside a windowed process (#616); null otherwise.
// `common/applications.js`'s `view()`, masked by `withoutSecrets()`.
// `recordFromAttributes()` gives every row of federation.js's SCHEMA a member:
// the single-valued as a string, the multi-valued as a list.
// `SpiffeRegistry.recordFromEntry()`, with `selectorTexts` from the view.
// `SpiffeRegistry.agentFromEntry()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `persistence.status()`, whole, on `GET /ldap/service`. Its nested reports are
// each module's own: `persistence_minted.js`, `used_assertions.js`,
// `persistence_replication.js`, `persistence_tiered.js`, `secrets.js`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The nine `/admin-api/ldap/*` pages, `ldap/ldap_server.js`'s views.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A PROTOCOL SETTINGS PAGE: `AdminConsole.protocolSettingsJson()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `config.js`'s `describe()`.
// `AdminConsole.configSettingsJson()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// KERBEROS.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `principals.kerberosRealmOf()`.
// `krbtgtState()`, and `rotationView()` over it.
// The Kerberos page's `status`: `kerberosPreauthStatusBlock()`, which is
// `Krb5Fast.policy()` (or its stand-in with `note`) plus the krbtgt and
// `Krb5Pkinit.policy()`.
// `mintServiceKeys()`, what `create-service` and `rotate-service` hand over.
// `dropPrevious()`, what the two `drop-previous-*` actions answer.
// `resetPersonKeytab()`: `personKeytab()`'s answer and what the reset adds.
// A refused password, `credentials.preparePassword()`'s words for why.
// `Krb5Trusts.viewOf()`, and `decorate()`'s three on the page's rows.
// The keys a generated trust key shows once, base64 by enctype number; each
// side is absent where its key was not generated.
// `kerberosTrustsAction()`: `{ ok, trustRealm, action }` over the library's.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TLS: the truststore and the connection.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `describeCertificate()`.
// `common/revocation_status.js`'s `summarise()` and `decide()`. A link is one
// certificate of the chain, with the route that answered about it copied on by
// `applyRouteResult()`.
// `tls_server.js`'s `connectionView()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FOUR CLAIM SETS: `AdminViews.claimSetsJson()` and `claimsAction()`.
//
// **`ClaimSets`'s members are all sent on every healthy read**, because
// `tests/protocol/admin_api.js` asserts each documented member of that schema
// is in a live reply; the `ClaimEntry` component stays `admin_api_spec.ts`'s,
// because the replace action's request body refers to it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `checkClaimEntries()`: a typed claim, or an attribute claim (#94), with what
// its kind of set adds, and `claimId` on the Kerberos page's rows.
// `ClaimAttributes.catalogueRows()`, with `pacClaimId` on the Kerberos page.
// One value of `subjectClaimsFor()`'s report, or of
// `kerberosPacAttributeValues()`.
// `GroupClaims.groupsOf()`.
// `claimsRequestJson()`, what `userinfoClaimsJson()` adds: OIDC Core 5.5.
// Every action of the claims, UserInfo, SAML attributes and PAC resources:
// `claimsAction()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SAML: the assertion settings, and the two profiles' registers.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `pagedRows()`'s own paging object, which these two pages send as it is
// rather than through `pagingJson()`, and so with `offset`.
// `consumedMetadataOf()`.
// One answer for the list and for `?sp=`: the two share no required member,
// and a model with top-level members is what `protocolEndpoints` is added to.
// The drill-down.
// The drill-down.
// `applications.updateApplication()` and its neighbours, which hand back the
// whole `view()`, and `saml2Action()`'s register.
// `SpMetadata.consume()`, what uploading, refreshing and importing metadata
// answer; here `application` is the identifier rather than the view.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SCIM: the surface, the monitor and the outbound clients (#552).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `ScimAuth.describe()`'s schemes.
// `admin_stats.js`'s `scimSnapshot()`.
// `scimMonitorSnapshot()`.
// `outbound_delivery.ts`'s `view()` and the SCIM client's `viewExtra`.
// `ScimClients.rowOf()`: an application's fields as the client's own members,
// never a credential, and what `view()` lays over it.
// `acrossCells()`'s row per cell.
// `ScimClients.view()`, and `acrossCells()`'s `cellId`.
// `ScimClients.act()`: `actHere()`'s answer; with `cell` and `reachable` over
// it for an act that named a cell; and one row per cell for an act that named
// none.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE DIRECTORY TAG'S THREE REGISTERS: attribute sources (#94), secret
// destinations (#221 P3) and post-login activities (#1111).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `AttributeSources.normalise()`, as `recordOf()` parses it back.
// `SecretDestinations.rowOf()`.
// A started activity. `started` on an action is the record as it is kept, its
// moments in milliseconds; on the page they are ISO strings, or null.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The models, by operation.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Runs once per operation while this table is built, as the kit's helpers do,
// so no Entering/Leaving pair: a line per model would drown that log.
// The operations whose row names a schema keep that name, because a test or
// a reader of the document may read it. `PageSettings` is not among them:
// other families' pages name it too, and the Kerberos page's differs by
// `status`, so those five are operations below.
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESPONSE MODELS (#1353): XACML, Policies, Delegation, UMA.
//
// `operations` is keyed by operationId, each value naming the statuses that
// have a model of their own; `components` replaces a named schema of
// `admin_api_spec.ts`. `common/response_model_kit.ts` says how a model is
// written and `mgmt-api/response_models.ts` how these are applied.
//
// **WRITTEN FROM THE CODE THAT BUILDS EACH ANSWER, NOT FROM WHAT WAS SEEN.** An
// answer these refuse is not sent, so a member a rare branch sends has to be
// here: a refused act's `holders`, the windowed consent page's `search`, a PDP
// status's `detail`, a person's picture's `signed-in` line. The comment above
// each group names the function whose return it states; a member added there
// owes a line here, or that answer becomes a 500.
//
// **THREE THINGS THAT LOOK WRONG AND ARE NOT.**
//
//   * A timestamp in the delegation pictures is a nullable number because
//     `user_graph.ts` folds with `Math.max(edge.lastAt, family.lastAt)`, and a
//     family with no `lastAt` is NaN, which JSON.stringify writes as null.
//   * A policy profile lists its fields by name, and its `defaults` and
//     `sources` are maps. Each kind's `FIELDS` table is its vocabulary, so the
//     profile is closed over it; `defaults` and `sources` are the same table
//     read as key and value, where a second copy of the list says nothing
//     more. **A new row in a kind's `FIELDS` owes a name in the list below.**
//     A new KIND of policy owes a member of `POLICIES` and a save and reset
//     row in `operations`.
//   * The console-only members and the owner's projection take members away
//     from these after the view built them, so nothing here but an action's
//     `ok` is `required`.
//
// THE FIVE FREE-FORM MEMBERS: an AuthZEN access request's `subject`,
// `resource`, `action` and `context` (the caller's own JSON, bound at the
// denial), and UMA's `endpoints` (RFC 8414 metadata). Each states why.
//
// No function here logs, as `response_model_kit.ts` says of its own: each runs
// only while a document's tables are built, and a line per schema would drown
// that log.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WORDS USED THROUGHOUT.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One value of a setting or of a policy's field. A row's type decides which,
// and a `csv` row is parsed into a list.
// `pagingJson()` in `admin-core/admin_views.ts`.
// `sendJson()` adds this to the answer of a GET mirroring a Protocols page;
// `protocol_endpoints.ts`'s `EndpointRow`. `apply()` writes it over a model
// with `properties`; it is declared as well so a model read alone is true.
// A refusal of a GET, the words `mgmt-api/response_models.ts`'s ERROR uses:
// the registration loop's own paging and filter checks answer
// `{ ok: false, errors }`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A PAGE'S SETTINGS BLOCK: `configSettingsJson()` in `admin-ui/admin.ts`, whose
// rows are `describe()` in `common/config.js` and whose `context` is
// `settingsContext()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// XACML: `xacml/xacml_admin.ts`. Every view answers `noXacml()`'s two members
// in a process without that module, so each carries them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `serviceOwnPolicies()`: the two states and what that function adds to them.
// A field of the model's nodes as `editDataFor()` copies it: the primitives of
// whichever node a tree row names, every kind `xacml_xml.js` reads, and the
// three sub-objects it copies whole. `value` and `reference` are a Match's, and
// their own members are the expression's.
// `xacml.enforce()`'s three members, as `decideJson()` copies them.
// `{ code, message, detail }`: `detail` is what the throw named, and these are
// every second argument of `model.syntaxError()`, `processingError()` and
// `missingAttribute()` in `xacml/`.
// `xacml_monitor.ts`'s `snapshot()`: a row of `rows`, of `remoteRows` (which
// has `remote` and nulls for the four decisions), and UMA's own row.
// `PepRegistry.all()`'s row, with what `pepsJson()` adds.
// `PepTls.certificateOf()`, with `chainPem` taken off by `pepsJson()`.
// `FgaPolicies.ownedPolicies()`.
// `XacmlTemplates.catalogue()`.
// `editorJson()`: both branches, the second being "nothing to edit".
// `[problem]` where the document does not load, and `problem` may be null.
// XACML's PolicyIdentifierList, each policy named with its version.
// `OutboundTls.describe()`.
// `xacmlAction()` turns `{ ok, why }` into `{ ok, why, errors }` and passes the
// rest. `store.write()`'s refusal reaches a caller whole, so `problems`, which
// is null where the throw named none; `editorAction()` answers `what`.
// `PepTls.issue()`: the one answer holding a private key, handed over once.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ONE APPLICATION AS `applications.js`'s `view()` DRAWS IT, which is what
// `updateApplication()` answers and so what a delegated permission grant and a
// global consent answer. An entry's attribute names are data.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `updateApplication()`, what `AppPermissions` adds (only a message), and what
// `Consent` adds. The `revoke*` and `forget` answers carry no application.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DELEGATION: `common/delegation.js`, and `common/user_graph.ts` on top of it
// for one person's picture. One node and one edge model for both those and
// `app_permissions.ts`'s `graph()`, because a picture of any of them is drawn
// by the same layout, which is why that function keeps these shapes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `party()`.
// `credential()`.
// `foldCredentials()`, and `user_graph.ts`'s `foldOnto()`.
// `recordUnguarded()`.
// `chainList()`.
// `applicationList()`.
// `graph()`'s `nodeFor()` and its issuer box, what `user_graph.ts`'s
// `normaliseNode()` and `nodeFor()` add, and `app_permissions.ts`'s three.
// `graph()`'s three edge seeds (an act's, the issuer's, a configured pair's),
// `user_graph.ts`'s four (signed in, issued for, reaches, issued), and
// `app_permissions.ts`'s, which keeps its two empty folds.
// `user_graph.ts`'s `credentialsFor()`, both of its rows.
// `graph()`, with the three `graphFor()` adds, and `app_permissions.ts`'s
// `counts`.
// `delegationNodeLook()`, the issuer's box and everybody else's.
// `delegationChooser()`. A person's `names` may hold an undefined `presented`,
// which an array writes as null.
// `delegationFacts()`.
// `user_graph.ts`'s `userList()`, `rowFromStats()` and `foldDelegation()`.
// `delegationUserModel()`.
// `FLOWS` in `user_graph.ts`, and `flowRow()`'s row for one it does not know.
// `delegationApplicationModel()`.
// `common/delegation_policy.ts`'s `list()`, both pair rows.
// `delegationFlaggedPersons()` in `ldap/ldap_server.js`.
// `krb5_principals.js`'s `delegationPolicy()`.
// `TYPES` in `common/delegation.js`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE DELEGATED PERMISSION REGISTER: `app_permissions.ts`'s `register()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The handler answers the register with `graph` beside it.
// `permissionGroupsView()`: `application` decides the shape, and a name nobody
// holds is `group: null` and a 200. One model and not `oneOf`: the two answers
// share `application` and `counts`, and a union would report a refused member
// once per branch, naming the wrong branch half the time.
// Every group, with no `application`.
// The one group an `application` is in.
// `delegationView()`'s `json`, whose counts are `summary()`'s. The four `by`
// maps are keyed by a mechanism, a mode, an outcome and a protocol, and an
// act recorded under an unknown type is counted under it.
// What `delegationPageModel()` adds.
// `permissionsListStateOf()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE POLICIES: `policiesView()` and `policyKindMember()` in
// `admin-core/admin_views.ts`, over the seven modules `admin-core/
// policy_kinds.ts` registers. Each kind's profile is `read()` in its module:
// these members, and that module's `FIELDS` by key.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A `FIELDS` row with what the view reads beside it. The two that say why a
// row cannot be used are `authnPolicyExtras()`'s.
// `namedBy()` in each module; `policiesViewAsync()` adds `peopleComplete`.
// `policyKindMember()`'s row, and the authentication policy's, which
// `authnPolicyExtras()` writes over it from `AuthnPolicy.named()`.
// `AuthnPolicy.named()`, as one act answers it.
// `AttributePolicy.definitions()`.
// `passwordPolicyExtras()`.
// `authnPolicyExtras()`.
// `PasskeyClients.view()`.
// `attributePolicyExtras()`.
// What `policiesAction()` answers for a save and for a reset, of one kind.
// A refused act keeps everything the module's refusal carried
// (`Object.assign({}, result, ...)`), which is `holders` on a custom attribute
// somebody still holds.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONSENT: `consentPageViewAsync()` and `consentPageView()` over
// `common/consent.ts`'s `register()`. The two differ in `search` and in what
// `counts` says.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `recordedPageAsync()`: null, or the exact search over the holders.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AUTHZEN: `authzen/authzen_admin.ts`'s `view()` and `act()`, and
// `AccessRequests.viewOf()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `callerOf()`, and `view()`'s two rows for a name it could not locate.
// A write the registry refuses is passed up as it is, so an application's
// `updateApplication()` refusal is `{ ok, errors }` and is covered already.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// UMA: `uma/uma_console.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `UmaOwnerPolicy.specOf()` and `withLabel()`.
// `ownerView()`: `{ found: false, asked }`, or the owner.
// `UmaOwnerPolicy.view()`, both branches.
// `whatIf()`, all three of its answers.
// `act()`. `application` here is the identifier, not `APPLICATION`.
// `UmaClaims.answer()` hands back the stored row as it is: `savePending()`'s
// members, whose `permissions` are the protocol's own two names.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE OPERATIONS.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The same model on several operations. Each action's `'200'` also serves its
// `'400'`: `Kit.applyModels()` says so, and each answers `ok` and `errors`.
// Named because `admin_api.ts`'s rows name them. None is a request body.
// The view is `DelegationList`; the gate may refuse a read 400.
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESPONSE MODELS (#1353): Service, Monitoring, Configuration, Trust realms,
// Persistence, Cluster, Metrics, Audit log, Risk, Devices.
//
// `operations` is keyed by operationId, each value naming the statuses that
// have a model of their own; `components` replaces a named schema of
// `admin_api_spec.ts`. `common/response_model_kit.ts` says how a model is
// written and `mgmt-api/response_models.ts` how these are applied.
//
// **EVERY MEMBER HERE WAS READ OFF THE CODE THAT BUILDS IT**, every branch, and
// an answer that fails its model is not sent. So where a member's type depends
// on a store, a mode or a provider, the union is written out; a time that is a
// number in one view and a string in another is each in its own place, not one
// shared `TIME`, because which one it is is what a client has to know.
//
// **NO `enum` ON A MEMBER WHOSE VALUES ARE ANOTHER MODULE'S TABLE**, unless the
// values were counted here: a value added to that table would otherwise turn
// every answer carrying it into a 500. `SETTING.source` is the one written out,
// and it is the case for the rule: the old schema's list had no `realm`, which
// is `config.js`'s first answer inside a realm.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// **AN ACTION'S 400 TAKES ITS 200's MODEL** (`Kit.applyModels()`), and a
// refusal can come from the gate as `{ error, error_description }` or from a
// module that says both (`devices.ts`'s `refuse()` sends `error` beside
// `errors`). So every action here declares those two as well, rather than one
// of them turning a refusal into a 500.
// What a setting can hold once `config.js`'s `TYPES` has parsed it: a number
// for int and port, a boolean for bool, a list for csv, otherwise a string. A
// `dflt` is the table's own literal, or what its function answers, so it is the
// same set and null.
// `admin_views.ts`'s `pagingJson()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A SETTING AS `config.js`'s `describe()` SENDS IT.
//
// **`legacyEnv` IS NOT DECLARED, ON PURPOSE**: `describe()` still sends it for
// a row that has one, and no row has had one since #523, so it never reaches
// JSON. `tests/protocol/admin_api.js` holds that every documented member
// appears on a row, so declaring it fails that test; the next row carrying one
// must put the member back here, in the same change, or every settings answer
// 500s.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `admin.ts`'s `settingsContext()`.
// `admin.ts`'s `configSettingsJson()`: the `settings` member of every page that
// owns settings.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A SECRET'S SOURCE, `common/secrets.js`'s `describeSecret()`. The KEK and the
// database password are both one. `where` is what the nine providers'
// `describe()` say between them; a value is never in it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /persistence`'s `status`: `persistence.js`'s `status()` and what it asks
// each part of the store.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `persistence_replication.js`'s `status()`: the store's, and a tiered store's
// global database's beside it. `lastTrim` carries what the store's
// `purgeChangeLog()` answered, or `error`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /cluster`'s `status`: `admin.ts`'s `clusterStatusBlock()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The cluster's own view of a node's caches is `cache_registry.js`'s packed row
// (`snapshotRowOf()`), an array because it travels on every heartbeat.
// `cluster.js`'s `status()`.
// `cells_admin.ts`'s `clusterSummaryHere()`, as another cell answers it.
// Another cell's own answer, relayed. A cell may be on another build while a
// fleet is upgraded, and one member this build has not heard of would otherwise
// take down this cell's whole cluster page (#1353).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `PageSettings` is what `admin.ts`'s `protocolSettingsJson()` answers for
// every page in its table, across families. Only the two rows that have a
// status block carry `status`, and it is one of those two.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /config-dump`: `admin-core/config_dump.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /config`: `admin.ts`'s `configJson()` over `config.js`'s `snapshot()`.
// Every member below is on every answer: `tests/protocol/admin_api.js` holds
// that, and reads `groups.items.properties.settings.items` directly, so the
// row stays inline here rather than behind a `$ref`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The four actions of `admin_actions.ts`'s `configAction()`, and what a refusal
// from `config.js` adds to them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /realms`: `admin_views.ts`'s `realmsJson()` and `realmJson()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `tls/listeners.js`'s `status()`.
// `realms.js`'s `retiringState()`.
// One model per action would say the same thing five times: what any of them
// adds, including what a refusal from `realms.js` carries. `retiring` is the
// refusal of a removal already under way; `bootstrap`, `password` and
// `username` are a created realm's, and the last two only in product mode.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /listeners`: `listeners_admin.ts`'s `listenersView()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A listener's own TLS values, which `listener_map.js` keeps as given after
// checking each against its base setting: the names are `config.js`'s
// `PER_LISTENER_SETTINGS`, so they are data here.
// `listener_map.js`'s `problem()`, drawn with its code for the operator who
// set what is wrong (#1355's exception). It was modelled as a string, so a
// listener setting with a problem answered 500 on the page that reports it.
// What `config.js`'s `setOverride()` and `realms.js`'s answer, both.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /mode`: `common/mode.js`'s `report()`. A requirement's columns are
// written in that table as the sentences they are.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /encryption`: `encryption_admin.ts`'s `encryptionJson()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The handler's 202, or its refusal.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /cells`: `cells_admin.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /caches`: `caches_admin.ts`'s `cachesJson()`, without `paging`, which
// `publicJson()` removes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /sts-metadata`: `sts_metadata.ts`'s `pageJson()`. An `ENDPOINTS` row may
// carry `coverage`, and a `specs` row `what`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Its metadata members, not settings: RFC 8414 members can be objects
// (`mtls_endpoint_aliases`), and `setMember()` takes any JSON on purpose.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /openapi.json`, and the account API's through `GET /account-api`. The
// inside of an OpenAPI document is OpenAPI's vocabulary rather than this
// service's, and what the operations in it say is the very table this file is
// applied to, so its members are the one honest free-form.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /admin-api`: the handler's own, `admin_api.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RISK: `risk_admin.ts`, over `risk_datasets.ts`, `risk_terms.ts`,
// `risk_engine.ts` and `risk_store.ts`.
//
// **AN ASSESSMENT COMES IN TWO SHAPES**: the memory store hands back what the
// engine wrote, and the database hands back its columns, so the row's model is
// their union and neither is required. `datasets` on it is a map of dataset id
// to the version read, with `stale` and `attributions` beside them in the same
// object, as the engine writes it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `store.listVersions()`: the memory row, or `riskVersionFrom()`'s columns.
// **`parameters` IS A BAG THIS SERVICE WRITES AS A VERSION PROGRESSES AND KEEPS
// IN THE DATABASE** (`attributionUrl`, `termsDigest`, `progressRows`, `mdsNo`,
// `signatureOverride`...), so a row an older build wrote may carry a member
// this one does not: a map, its values typed, rather than a closed list.
// `risk_terms.ts`'s `status()`: the provider's row of `PROVIDERS` and what was
// said of it.
// A hit in a range table: `riskRangeFrom()`'s columns, or what `parseRow()`
// made of the file's line in the memory store, with `dataset` added.
// One line of an assessment's `signals`. The first is always `model`, the rest
// each one thing that moved the score.
// `RiskStore.metricsOf()`. Every `by*` is a count per value that a row held,
// and a series point a count per level, so they are maps.
// What any of the risk actions answers, including a refused import's `dataset`
// and `version`, and an upload's. `duplicate` is a version already recorded;
// `serial` and `overridden` are a FIDO BLOB's.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GEOLOCATION: `geolocation_admin.ts`'s `geoView()`. `shown()` withholds a
// count below `risk.geoMinimumCount`, so a count is nullable everywhere.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MAIL: `mail_admin.ts` over `common/mail.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `mail.ts`'s `view()`. A captured message's `text` and `html` only when one
// message is asked for.
// `verified` is `startVerification()`'s answer for an address already verified.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A REFUSAL OF A READ. A GET's row declares only its 200, so what its door says
// to a query parameter outside a closed set is `default`; this names the same
// words for the ones that are refused that way.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DEVICES: `devices_admin.ts` over `common/devices.ts`. A key's values are
// stored on its entry as JSON and read back with `JSON.parse`, so `DEVICE_KEY`
// is what `prepareKey()` writes; `revocation` is `attestation_revocation.ts`'s
// verdict.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The public members of whichever key type it is, which are JOSE's.
// `mdmFeed()`. A row's refusal is `setCompliance()`'s.
// `sendJson()` adds this to the GET mirroring a Protocols page, and
// `response_models.ts` declares it again from its own table.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SCHEDULER: `scheduler_admin.ts` over `cluster/scheduler.ts`. `runView()`
// is the one shape of a run; a run's `params` is what the person who asked for
// it sent, which is whatever that job reads.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT EVERY NODE SNAPSHOT SAYS: `node_snapshots.ts`'s `read()`, under both the
// worker pools and node health.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NODE HEALTH: `node_health_admin.ts`. A figure this node could not read is
// null beside a sentence, not a zero, so every figure is nullable, and an
// `available: false` view has only `unavailableText`. `withEcs()` overlays the
// ECS endpoint's on what the cgroup said.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What ECS's metadata endpoint said under `Limits`, whose names are its own.
// `collectGarbage()`, whose answer is the same at 200, at 404 (`threadIds`) and
// at 500, where the one isolate asked for could not collect: a 500 carrying the
// rows is why this is not the API's plain refusal.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DATABASE: `database_admin.ts` over each driver's `metrics()`. A probe's rows
// are the database engine's own statistics views.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SECRETS: `secrets_admin.ts` over `secrets.js`'s `storeReport()`. A probe's
// `data` is what the secret store's own API answered, scrubbed of values.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `debugger_server.ts`'s `status()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// KEYS: `crypto_metadata.ts`'s `keysJson()`, `signing_rotation.ts`'s
// `rotationView()`, `signing_history.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /status`: `admin.ts`'s `consoleJson()`. `dashboardJson()` adds what only
// the console draws, and the registration loop removes it from this answer.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `meJson()`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /metrics`: `admin_stats.js`'s `snapshot()`, with `signOnSessionRows()`.
// The members at the top are all on every answer, which
// `tests/protocol/admin_api.js` holds. What is inside a claim is
// `CLAIM_ENTRY`'s.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `GET /crypto`: `crypto_metadata.ts`'s `cryptoJson()`. Every list in it is
// read from the module that performs the algorithm, which is why a value here
// is never an `enum`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE AUDIT LOG: `audit.js`'s `record()` writes one row, and `admin_views.ts`'s
// `auditView()` answers them. A row's `detail` is whatever the several hundred
// call sites each record about their own act.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PASSKEYS: `common/passkey_report.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AUDIT EXPORT: `common/audit_export.ts`. `publicDest()` copies the stored
// destination without its token and its secret headers, so its members are
// `prepare()`'s; the ones a request can name a number or a text for take both.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SIGN-IN APPEARANCE: `branding_api.ts` over `common/branding.ts`. A refused
// logo carries its own `status`, at the 400, 413 or 415 it says.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE DELIVERIES: `outbound_delivery.ts`'s `view()` and each kind's
// `viewExtra()`.
//
// **THE INTER-CELL KIND ANSWERS ITS RAW ROWS** (`cell_deliveries.ts`'s `rows()`
// and `retry()` return the queue's rows, not `view()`), so a time is a number
// there and text everywhere else, and `body`, `updatedAt`, `fenceAt` and the
// digests appear. Reported on #1353; the model says what is sent.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESPONSE MODELS (#1353): Federation, OpenID Federation, PKI, ACME, SCEP, EST,
// SPIFFE, Shared Signals, CAEP, RISC, GNAP.
//
// `operations` is keyed by operationId, each value naming the statuses that
// have a model of their own; `components` replaces a named schema of
// `admin_api_spec.ts`. `common/response_model_kit.ts` says how a model is
// written and `mgmt-api/response_models.ts` how these are applied.
//
// **A VIEW THAT MIRRORS A PROTOCOLS PAGE IS ONE `closed()`, NEVER A
// `oneOf()`.** `ResponseModels.apply()` adds `protocolEndpoints` to a model
// that has `properties`, and a union has none: a list and a detail written as
// a union would refuse the member `sendJson()` adds. So those views' models are
// the union of their members in one object, and only `required` is left out.
//
// **WHAT A PARTNER SENT IS NOT THIS SERVICE'S TO TYPE.** `PARTNER_VALUE` and
// the other free-form uses below are each a value a foreign document carried
// and this service keeps or relays as it arrived, and a strict type there would
// turn one partner's odd document into a 500 on an operator's own page.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A moment some rows hold as an ISO string and others as milliseconds.
// A value that is any scalar a setting or a counter may hold.
// The paging object `AdminViews.pagingJson()` writes.
// One row `common/config.js`'s `describe()` writes. `default` and `value` take
// the type of the setting, a list included.
// `AdminConsole.configSettingsJson()`: a page's settings, or null where the
// console has not filled the slot.
// A row of `protocolEndpoints`, which `sendJson()` adds to a GET mirroring a
// Protocols page. `apply()` writes `admin_api_spec.ts`'s copy over this one,
// and only the SPIFFE sockets' rows carry `listening`: see SPIFFE, below.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FEDERATION. A relationship's attributes are `federation.js`'s SCHEMA, which
// is why the record is closed over the names rather than a map of them: they
// are this service's own schema, and a new one owes a row here beside its row
// there. `recordFromAttributes()` gives every single-valued one a string and
// every multi-valued one a list.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The attributes `federation.js`'s schema marks `sensitive`, left out of a
// write's answer by `AdminApi.federationAnswer()`.
// A directory entry as `ldap_server.js`'s `entryObject()` hands it over. Its
// attribute names are data: an entry carries what an `ldapmodify` gave it.
// A relationship as a write answers it, `AdminActions.answeredFederation()`:
// the attributes and where the entry is. NOT the credentials and NOT the entry
// that holds them (#1354), and this model is what holds that rule: a handler
// that answers one again is refused rather than sent.
// A public JWK of an encryption key, or a partner-facing key row. A JWK's
// members depend on its key type (RFC 7517 section 4) and the AKP ones are
// post-quantum, so they are listed and a member the crypto engine adds is a
// model change.
// `SsfTransmitters.view()`: what this service holds of a partner's stream. The
// members are this service's; `config` and `stream` hold what the partner
// answered, so their values are `PARTNER_VALUE`.
// What a stream-updated said the partner's stream now is (SSF 1.0, 8.1.5).
// The list.
// One of them: `found`, then `federationRow()`'s members (`ready`, above, is
// the list's count there and this row's boolean here), then the rest.
// The sensitive attributes read `(set — not returned)` or empty, the one
// multi-valued one among them (`fedEncryptionKey`) included.
// What a GET answers when  refuses a parameter.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PKI. `admin-ui/pki_admin.ts`'s `pkiJson()` and `pkiAction()`, over
// `common/pki.js`, `common/pki_revocation.js`, `common/person_assertions.js`
// and `common/pki_authoring.ts`. `classify()` there writes `pqc` onto every
// object under `chain`, `tree` and `workbench` that carries a certificate, so
// those rows all declare it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A tier of a chain, `describeTier()` and `describeChain()` in one.
// A workbench draft: one member per field of `pki_authoring.ts`'s FIELDS, whose
// names include one per X.509 key usage, EKU and Netscape bit from the crypto
// engine's tables, so the keys are read from those tables and are data here.
// `describeObject()`; and the record an issue answers with, which adds the
// public keys. Neither private key: `PkiAdmin.issuedAnswer()` (#1359).
// One key pair of a person, as `person_assertions.js` stores and reads it.
// `CertificateDetails.describe()`. The extension's decoded value is free-form:
// the certificate may be anybody's and each extension's shape is its own ASN.1
// module's, as the crypto engine's parser hands it over.
// The list, or with `certificate` one of them; `realm` is in both.
// `pkiAction()`. Every refusal there carries `why` beside `errors`,
// `issue-certificate`'s carries `draft` as well, and the management API adds
// `workbench` to any answer holding a `draft`.
// Null for a key JOSE has no name for, or a KEM's, which signs nothing.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SPIFFE. `admin-core/admin_views.ts`'s `spiffe*Json()`, over
// `spiffe/spiffe_ca.ts`, `spiffe_registry.ts`, `spiffe_auth.ts` and
// `spiffe_server.ts`.
//
// **WRITTEN AS A ONE-MEMBER UNION, WHICH IS DELIBERATE.** These operations (and
// ACME's, EST's and SCEP's reads) mirror `/admin/cert-issuance`, whose
// `protocolEndpoints` holds SPIFFE's socket rows, each carrying `listening`.
// `admin_api_spec.ts`'s `PROTOCOL_ENDPOINT_ROWS` does not declare that member,
// and `ResponseModels.apply()` writes it over a model that has `properties`,
// which would refuse every such answer once a SPIFFE socket is bound. A union
// has no `properties`, so `ENDPOINT_ROW`, above, is what is held to.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// An entry's attribute names are data: a directory entry carries what an
// `ldapmodify` gave it, beside the schema's.
// The list.
// One of them, by `entry`.
// The list.
// One of them, by `agent`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ACME, EST AND SCEP: `acme/acme_console.ts`, `est/est_console.ts` and
// `scep/scep_console.ts` over `common/cert_enrollment.ts` and
// `common/enrollment_monitor.ts`. Each read mirrors `/admin/cert-issuance`, and
// the one-member union is SPIFFE's reason, above. Its monitor does not.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a monitor's counters began, on this process.
// `core.refuse()`'s `{ ok, status, errors, why }` travels unchanged through
// EST's console.
// One URL per EST operation, by the operation's name.
// RFC 8894 section 3.2.1.2's messageType numbers, keyed by the number.
// Counted per name, where ACME's and EST's are tables of the same counts.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GNAP. `gnap/gnap_console.ts` over `gnap_grants.ts`, `gnap_tokens.ts` and
// `gnap_monitor.ts`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RFC 9635 section 9's discovery document. An authorization server's overrides
// are written over the defaults, so a member may be whatever an administrator
// gave it (`authorization_servers.ts`'s `capabilitiesOf()`).
// A counter row: one number per event of `gnap_monitor.ts`'s EVENTS, with
// `lastAt`, `lastEvent`, `formats` and `errors` beside them; the counters'
// names are that table's, and a new event is a row there and no change here.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SHARED SIGNALS, CAEP AND RISC. `ssf/ssf.ts`'s three reports and three action
// handlers, `ssf/ssf_dead_letter_report.ts`, `ssf/ssf_receivers.ts` and
// `ssf/ssf_transmitters.ts`. A stream's counters and an event's counts are
// counted per name, so they are maps of numbers.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `transmit()` and `transmitterVerification()`. **It carries the signed token
// and its claims**: the report on #1353 says where.
// What both vocabularies say of a stream, and of a receiver.
// One session, by `session`.
// One account, by `account`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OPENID FEDERATION. `oidfed/oidfed.ts`'s `view()` and `act()`, with
// `oidfed_admin.ts`'s `pageLists()`.
//
// **WHAT A STATEMENT CARRIES IS THAT STATEMENT'S.** An Entity Configuration's
// metadata is keyed by entity type and each type's members are its own
// specification's, a resolved one's is a partner's as resolved, and a metadata
// policy's operators are OpenID Federation 1.1 section 6.1's, extensible by
// `metadata_policy_crit`. This service types what it adds around them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// #1360. Paranoid PKI mode, the signer truststore and what is pinned. No member
// here carries an error code: those are recorded, never sent.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//@@NEXT@@
//
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESPONSE MODELS (#1353): Users, Groups, Multi-factor authentication, Admin
// roles, Roles, Sign-out, Sessions, Ownership, TOTP MFA, Recovery codes,
// WebAuthn.
//
// `operations` is keyed by operationId, each value naming the statuses that
// have a model of their own; `components` replaces a named schema of
// `admin_api_spec.ts`. `common/response_model_kit.ts` says how a model is
// written and `mgmt-api/response_models.ts` how these are applied.
//
// **WHO THE MEMBERS CAME FROM.** Every list below was read off the code that
// builds the answer, not off a recording: `AdminActions.usersAction()` and its
// helpers, `AdminViews.userDetailJson()`, `Credentials`, `Logout`, and the
// directory's `groupsFor()`. A member a model misses is an answer that is not
// sent, so where a branch is rare it is listed all the same.
//
// **FOUR NAMED SCHEMAS ARE READ BY A TEST** that asks each documented member
// to be in a live reply (`tests/protocol/admin_api.js`, *the schemas match the
// replies*): `UserList`, `GroupList`, `GroupDetail` and `GroupDetail.group`.
// So those four hold exactly what a healthy reply carries, and the answers a
// healthy reply is not are models of their own beside them (`GROUPS_ABSENT`,
// `GROUP_MISSING`, `GROUP_FOR_OWNER`, `USER_UNKNOWN`), joined in the
// operation's `oneOf`, rather than conditional members on the named schema.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A moment some rows hold as an ISO string and others as milliseconds, and a
// directory that may hold either.
// `AdminViews.pagingJson()`.
// `common/config.js`'s `describe()`, a row; `default` and `value` take the type
// of the setting, a list included.
// `AdminConsole.configSettingsJson()`: a page's settings, or null where the
// console has not filled the slot. A `sharedWith` value is the pages that also
// draw a group, each `{ path, label }`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A directory entry. Its attribute NAMES are data: whatever is on the entry,
// less the secrets `withoutSecrets()` masks. A value is a list as the store
// holds it; the lone string is what an older copy of an entry carries.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Which rule made it a group, and its members resolved: what the group
// writers add to the entry they hand back.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A PERSON'S FACTORS.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What `Credentials.factorHolderRow()` and the roster row have in common.
// A composite statement's parts (`algorithmsOf()`): each the same facts of
// one member, under the format it was in.
// `AttestationRevocation.consult()`'s verdict on a certificate chain.
// `authn/webauthn_attestation.ts`'s `assess()`, stored on the key as it was.
// RFC 4998, `common/passkey_evidence.ts`.
// `common/attestation_revocation.ts`'s verdict on the chain.
// `Credentials.addKey()`'s stored record, as `keysOf()` reads it back, and the
// three things `userPageData()` adds to each. The keys' `publicKeyJwk` is a
// COSE key turned into a JWK: its members are the algorithm's, closed here
// across the four key families a passkey may be.
// Two refusals a sign-in leaves on the key (`noteKeyPolicyRefused()`, and the
// attestation recheck's): the policy's by its kind, whose name is data.
// `AdminViews.mfaJson()`'s key: a description, no public key.
// `Credentials.mechanismsFor()`, with the key rows `userPageData()` writes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A PERSON'S LISTS, each answered alone by an operation of its own and inside
// `page.mfa` on the drill-down.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A verified claim's value is the entry's, and `address` is an object.
// `wallet-proof`, `person`, `current-key-proof` and `new-key-proof`.
// A device key: `common/devices.ts`'s header. Its proof is one of the realm's
// words, and its material is the key's own.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SIGN-ON SESSIONS AND WHAT WAS ISSUED ON THEM.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `AdminStats.recordJwt()`: the claims and facts of a token, with its state.
// `recordArtifact()`, with each of its four callers' details: an assertion, a
// ticket, an SVID and a credential.
// `revokeArtifact()`: an artifact can be disowned as a token can.
// `common/audit.js`'s row: an audit event, on the drill-down's attempts.
// `ServiceAccounts.of()`: the facts on the entry.
// `AdminViews.serviceAccountJson()`: those facts as a page reads them.
// `PersonEditor.editorFor()`.
// `AdminViews.personCredentialsState().json`.
// The same state as the drawing page reads it (`userPageData()`'s `keyPairs`).
// What the drawing page reads (`userPageData()`). `mfa` is `{ storable }`
// alone where no credential store is installed.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE LIST, AND THE ONE IDENTITY. Named: a test reads `UserList`'s members.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A name nobody knows is an answer about the identity, 200.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SESSIONS, ACROSS EVERY FAMILY THAT HOLDS ONE.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `note` says why, where `logout/logout.ts` is not in this process.
// `Logout.terminate()`, and what it hands the page to make a browser do.
// `inventoryFor()`: what a family held, what was cut, and a collector's
// failure. The list of families with no person named has none of them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GROUPS. `GroupList`, `GroupDetail` and its `group` are read by a test.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `groupsFor()` reads the whole list whichever it was asked, so a drill-down
// carries these two as well.
// A drill-down says the directory's cap; the list's answer does not, and
// `tests/protocol/admin_api.js` reads `GroupList`, so it stays off that one.
// No directory is loaded in this process (`AdminViews.groupsJson()`).
// `?group=` naming nothing, or an entry that is not a group: an answer, 200.
// What an owner of the group is answered (`OWNER_READS`' projection).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CONSOLE'S ROLES.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ROSTER OF SECOND FACTORS, AND THE THREE MECHANISM PAGES.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The FIDO Metadata Service, as the mechanism page adds it.
// `sendJson()` adds it to a successful read of a Protocols page.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE RESOURCE ROLES, ON `/admin/roles`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `AdminViews.rolesPreview()`, with what the role PEP's `preview()` answers.
// The two verdicts `IssuanceAnswer` declares for a question a preview does
// not ask today; a member of the answer all the same.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE ACTIONS ANSWER. `reason` and `problems` are what a refusal from a
// domain module may carry beside `errors`; a '200' here also serves the '400'.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `AccountState.endEverything()`: counts, not the rows `terminate()` said.
// A READ'S '400' IS THE REFUSAL AND NOT THE READ: left to itself a status
// borrows the '200' (`applyModels()`), and a query the handler refuses answers
// `{ ok, errors }`, which no view's model holds.
// What `?invent=` adds, and why it did not.
// `?account=`, an owner's read.
// A name somebody holds, and what the attribute policy still asks for.
// The attribute names a create refused because a person does not
// have them (STS-LDAP-0047). Absent, the refusal is something else
// (#1404).
// What a refusal from the module that owns the object may say.

// The notes below sat on the route rows in `mgmt-api/admin_api.ts`.
//
// A kind with named profiles takes another name
// (#1236: said only of the kinds without them).
// A row marked `optional` (the passkey policy's
// recoveryAssurance, #724) may be left out and keeps its default.
// In words, as `/me`'s is: the banner every console page draws is
// not a page, and the send it answers for is `POST /admin/broadcast`.
// `true` from a script, `true` or `on` from the console's
// checkbox (#472).
// Both served on the `admin` listener AND kept on `main`,
// advertised there. An example that took either off the
// listener its caller is on locks that caller out:
// sts_admin_api_operations replays every example and reads the
// resource back through the same port, and sts_admin_console's
// realm switcher then lands on that realm's console through
// `main` (both 404, STS-TLS-0047, on 2026-10-07).
// NO CONSOLE FORM POSTS AS A FEED, and saying it mirrored the device
// page made #86's console gate hold the feed's fields on that page's
// forms. An administrator's counterpart is `set-compliance`, which
// mirrors the page itself; this line names it without a `POST
// /admin…` for `registerConsoleClosedSets()` to read.
// THE EXAMPLE IS WHAT DRIVES THIS OPERATION IN THE SUITE.
// `sts_admin_api_operations.js` walks every GET and every POST
// that carries one; an operation with no example is covered by
// nothing and reported by nothing, which is what its ledger
// refuses. `pem` with no password is chosen deliberately — it is
// the one combination that needs nothing set up and hands back a
// file every tool reads.
// A SERVICE ACCOUNT FROM THE START (#221): checked before the
// person is created, so a refused one leaves nobody behind.
// THE HOME CELL (#98 D1), which `cell_placement.ts`'s
// creation claim reads before this operation runs: a cell
// other than the serving one is relayed there whole. It was
// read there and refused HERE — not a member of this schema —
// at the cell it was relayed to, so no creation naming
// another cell could succeed until the `cells` mode's first
// run (2026-09-28, tests/protocol/sts_cells_routing.js).
// THE NEW-USER FORM'S BOXES (#446): the static console sends
// /admin/users/new's field grid here as it is, and
// `userFieldsFrom()` merges them over `attributes`.
// ACCEPTED AND IGNORED (#1135), so an older body is not
// refused by `additionalProperties: false`.
// BOTH SPELLINGS, because this action and
// `POST /admin-api/users/clear-totp` are ONE SWITCH reached two
// ways — and the users resource names the person `user`, as
// every other operation on it does. A schema that took one
// spelling here and the other there would refuse a caller that
// had merely followed the other path's example, with a message
// about a member of the request rather than about the person.
// See `clear-totp` above: one switch, two paths, one body
// shape.
// THE NEW-APPLICATION FORM'S BOXES (#446): the static console
// sends /admin/applications/new's field grid here as it is,
// and `applicationFieldsFrom()` merges them over `fields`.
// THE LIST IS READ OFF THE SCHEMA rather than typed here, and this
// and the one on `add` below were typed until 2026-08-25. Making
// the per-protocol identifiers multi-valued moved six names from
// this sentence to that one — and neither sentence noticed, which
// is the whole argument: a hand-written list of what an operation
// accepts is a second definition of the EDITABLE table, and it goes
// wrong silently in the document a caller trusts most.
// Not `oauthTokenEndpointAuthMethod` since 2026-10-01: it is
// a list now, written with `add` and `remove`.
// Read off the schema, for the reason `set` above gives.
// #564: the fine-grained mapping, written whole.
// `true` from a script, `true` or `on` from the console's
// checkbox, as the listeners mapping takes it.
// NOT required here: an unticked box posts nothing, and
// the handler's refusal (STS-FGA-0020) says what the
// validator's could not — what the reset would do.
// `true` from a script, `on` from the console's checkbox.
// THE EMPTY EXAMPLE IS WHAT DRIVES IT.
// `tests/protocol/sts_admin_api_operations.js` replays every POST
// that carries an example and then asserts that every documented
// operation was driven by something — so an action taking no
// parameters and declaring no example is covered by nothing and
// reported by nothing, which is what this one was until that ledger
// went red on it. `{}` is the shape `revoke-all` and `clear-store`
// use for the same reason.
// The handler tells a field that records what
// HAPPENED from one that does not exist, and a
// caller needs to know which (#86, see
// REFUSED_BY_HANDLER).

import "../lib/api_lib.tsp";

using IyaSts.ApiLib;

@apiDocument(#{
  `openapi`:"3.1.0",
  `info`:#{
    `title`:"IYA STS management API",
    `version`:"0.0.0",
    `description`:"The management API of IYA STS: everything the /admin console shows and everything it can change, over JSON, with no browser.\n\n**Every operation here requires an OAuth 2.0 access token, and this document says which scope each one needs.** Ask this service's own token endpoint for one: `grant_type=client_credentials` as the seeded client `sts-api-client` (its secret is `adminApi.clientSecret`), `scope=admin:read admin:write`, and **`resource=<this server>/admin-api`** — that last parameter is what puts this API in the token's `aud`, and a token audienced at anything else is refused however good it is. A GET needs `admin:read` and everything else needs `admin:write`; they are checked as a XACML access decision against the built-in ADMIN_READ and ADMIN_WRITE roles, so the requirement is stated in the same policy document as every other access decision in this service. **The credential is service-wide**: the token is verified against the DEFAULT realm's key and audienced without a realm prefix wherever it is presented, for the reason the console's two roles are groups in the default realm — a per-realm one would let anybody who can create a realm mint themselves an administrator. A trust realm's own administrators have a narrower token of their own: issued to that realm's `sts-api-client` client, accepted only under `/realm/<id>/admin-api`, and refused every service-wide operation. `adminApi.authRequired` turns this off and restores the open API exactly; what follows is what that means. **AND IT IS STILL WORTH STATING PLAINLY**: a holder of this token can revoke every token this service has issued and change what the next one contains. That is fine on a laptop or a compose network and is not fine on a public address, which was already true of /oauth2/token — it will mint a token for any username asked of it.\n\n**Many operations change what the protocol endpoints do**, rather than only reporting on them; four examples. Revoking a token is the same revocation RFC 7009's /oauth2/revoke performs, so introspection, UserInfo and the refresh grant all honour it immediately. A custom claim reaches every access token, ID Token and SAML assertion issued from then on. The credential claim set reaches every Verifiable Credential AND sweeps the embedded LDAP directory. And the verifier request reaches the dcql_query of the next OID4VP Authorization Request. Nothing already issued ever changes — a signed document cannot be reached into.\n\n**Every operation here mirrors a control on the /admin console and calls the same function behind it.** They are not two implementations of one idea: admin_api.js holds no opinion about what a revocation means that the console does not. The `mirrors` line on each operation says which control it is.\n\nIn the default memory persistence mode all state is in memory and dies with the process; `GET /admin-api/persistence` says what this process writes down.",
    `license`:#{`name`:"MIT"}
  },
  `servers`:#[#{`url`:"/",`description`:"This service."}],
  `tags`:#[
    #{
      `name`:"Service",
      `description`:"What this API is, its document, and the explorer that calls it."
    },
    #{`name`:"Shared Signals",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Credential status",
      `description`:"The status lists every issued Verifiable Credential names, and suspending, reinstating or revoking one: Monitoring → Verifiable Credentials (`/admin/vc/monitor`) in the console."
    },
    #{`name`:"Risk",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Devices",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Users",
      `description`:"Who it has authenticated, and what each of them holds."
    },
    #{
      `name`:"Metrics",
      `description`:"What this service has done since it started."
    },
    #{`name`:"Sessions",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Sign-out",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Groups",
      `description`:"The embedded LDAP directory's groups. A group here GRANTS NOTHING: no token, assertion, ticket or PAC carries one and no endpoint reads one."
    },
    #{`name`:"LDAP",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Admin roles",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Multi-factor authentication",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Tokens",
      `description`:"What has been issued, and the revocation of the three kinds that can be revoked."
    },
    #{`name`:"Trust realms",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Configuration",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"OAuth 2.0 / OIDC",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"OpenID4VCI",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"OpenID4VP",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"TOTP MFA",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Recovery codes",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"WebAuthn",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Kerberos",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Persistence",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Cluster",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"WS-Trust",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"WS-Federation",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"TLS",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Token lifetimes",
      `description`:"How long an access token, an ID Token and a refresh token issued here last, and how far out a clock may be before this service stops believing one of its own. Four of the settings under Configuration, behind a name that promises four rather than forty-nine — and a door that refuses a key it does not recognise instead of ignoring it."
    },
    #{`name`:"SAML assertions",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Custom claims",
      `description`:"What to add to every access token and ID Token issued from now on. The assertions are next door, under Custom SAML attributes."
    },
    #{`name`:"UserInfo claims",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Custom SAML attributes",
      `description`:"What to add to every SAML 2.0 and SAML 1.1 assertion issued from now on — WS-Trust's and WS-Federation's alike. The same store the custom claims are in, and the same two halves: a typed attribute and a directory one."
    },
    #{
      `name`:"Credential claims",
      `description`:"What an issued Verifiable Credential carries."
    },
    #{
      `name`:"Verifier request",
      `description`:"What this realm's OID4VP Verifier asks a wallet for."
    },
    #{`name`:"Federation",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"SAML 2.0",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"SAML 1.1",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Authorization servers",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Applications",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"XACML",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"GNAP",
      `description`:"The Grant Negotiation and Authorization Protocol (RFC 9635) and its resource server connections (RFC 9767): the authorization server of this realm, what the applications using it have done, and the two things an operator does to its state by hand."
    },
    #{`name`:"CAEP",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"RISC",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"SCIM",
      `description`:"The SCIM 2.0 provisioning endpoints under /scim/v2 — what they have been asked to do, and what they will and will not do. READ-ONLY here, and that is the parity rule holding rather than a gap: the console page has no form on it either, because everything about SCIM that can be changed is a configuration row. What SCIM WROTE is under Users and Groups, since it provisions into the same directory and has no store of its own."
    },
    #{`name`:"PKI",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Audit log",
      `description`:"What happened here, in order — history rather than state, and read-only. It carries no credential of any kind and has no clear operation: an erase control on an administrative surface would make an audit log unable to answer the one question it exists for."
    },
    #{`name`:"Delegation",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Roles",
      `description`:"Who holds a role, and what requires one. A role is a name a person, a GROUP or an APPLICATION can be mapped into, and holding one is what an ISSUANCE is decided on — which is the whole difference from Groups above, where a group grants nothing. The decision is made by the XACML PDP against a policy, never by an `if` in an issuance site, so a refusal is a document somebody can read. It is not Admin roles either: those are two directory groups that grant the /admin console and nothing else."
    },
    #{`name`:"Ownership",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"Policies",
      `description`:"The rules this realm holds a credential to — the password policy first: its minimum length, how many previous passwords may not be reused, and the symbol, uppercase and digit rules, stored as `cn=default,ou=passwordPolicies` in the directory and ENFORCED IN PRODUCT MODE at every door that sets a password. Not the XACML policy repository, which is under XACML."
    },
    #{`name`:"SPIFFE",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"ACME",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"EST",`description`:""},
    #{
      `name`:"SCEP",
      `description`:"The Simple Certificate Enrolment Protocol (RFC 8894) server of this realm: its Issuing CA and RA certificate, the single-use challenge passwords that authorize an enrollment, registered host names, the certificates issued over it, and what it has done."
    },
    #{`name`:"OpenID Federation",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Directory",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"UMA",`description`:""},
    #{`name`:"Monitoring",`description`:""}
  ],
  `security`:#[
    #{`oauth2`:#["admin:read","admin:write","admin:security"]},
    #{`bearerAuth`:#[]}
  ],
  `components`:#{
    `securitySchemes`:#{
      `oauth2`:#{
        `type`:"oauth2",
        `description`:"An access token from this service's own token endpoint. Ask as the seeded client `sts-api-client`, whose secret is the `adminApi.clientSecret` setting, and **send `resource=/admin-api`** with the request: OpenAPI has no field for RFC 8707's resource indicator and it is what puts this API in the token's `aud`, without which every call here is refused 401.",
        `flows`:#{
          `clientCredentials`:#{
            `tokenUrl`:"/oauth2/token",
            `scopes`:#{
              `admin:read`:"Read anything this API exposes (every GET).",
              `admin:write`:"Change anything this API can change (every other method), except a security change.",
              `admin:security`:"A security change, in place of admin:write: a Directory → Policies policy saved, reset or acted on, a policy named on an application, group or person, the Security Administrator's role granted or revoked, and the account of a person who holds it. Issued only to a subject holding SECURITY_ADMIN.",
              `admin:console`:"The admin console's own operations, under /admin-api/console (#454). Issued only to the console's client, sts-admin-console, through the ADMIN_CONSOLE role it confers.",
              `device:compliance`:"Report device compliance through POST /admin-api/device-compliance, and nothing else — the scope an MDM or posture feed holds (#164). Issued only to a client that declares it."
            }
          }
        }
      },
      `bearerAuth`:#{
        `type`:"http",
        `scheme`:"bearer",
        `bearerFormat`:"JWT",
        `description`:"The same token, pasted. Use this when you already hold one — from a launcher's `STS_ADMIN_API_TOKEN`, from the console's API explorer, or from a shell — rather than minting one here."
      }
    }
  },
  `x-iya-doc`:"management"
})
namespace IyaSts.Api.Management {
  

@js(#{
    `description`:"What the console's own action functions return, unchanged. `ok` is true when the operation was applied and false when it was refused; a refusal carries `errors` and is answered with HTTP 400. Successful replies carry `message` — the sentence the console would have shown — plus whatever the action produced: `claims` for a claim set, `selected` for the credential claim set, `requested` for the verifier request, `revoked` for a count.",
    `additionalProperties`:true
  }) @component model `ActionResult` {
    @js(#{`description`:"Whether the operation was applied."}) `ok`: boolean;
    @js(#{
      `description`:"What happened, in the words the console would have used. Present on success."
    }) `message`?: string;
    @js(#{`description`:"Why it was refused. Present when `ok` is false."}) `errors`?: (string)[]
  }

