@endpoint("/admin-api/users",
  "get",
  #{
    `operationId`:"getUsers",
    `summary`:"Everyone this service has authenticated, or one of them in full",
    `description`:"Without `user` it is the list. With it, one identity: the names they were seen under, how they authenticated each time, every sign-on session they hold with the tokens issued ON each of those, and their LDAP entry. A name this service has never seen answers 200 with `known: false` rather than 404 — it is an answer about the identity, not about the route.\n\nThe identity is a QUERY PARAMETER and not a path segment on purpose: the identities here contain the characters a path is made of (a Kerberos service principal is `HTTP/host`, a subject is a `urn:`), so /users/HTTP/web.example.com would be a two-segment path naming nobody.\n\nONE PAGE PARAMETER IS NOT IN THE LIST BELOW, because its name is data: each session's own token list is moved by `session-<the session id>Page`, so `?user=alice&session-8Qk3...Page=2` moves that block and no other, and each session in the reply answers with its own `tokensPaging`. It is named after the session rather than numbered so that the link still moves the same session after the list around it has changed, and it is paged at all because one browser session can hold most of the tokens this service remembers. OpenAPI cannot spell a parameter whose name is built at runtime, and a `session-{id}Page` in the list would generate a client that sends a literal `{id}` — so it is here in words instead.\n\n**The ?user= reply's `authenticationEvents`** (#955) are the audit log's authentication attempts by this person, newest first, across every application and protocol: `authentication`, `authentication.refused`, `session.refuse` and `session.refused`. A row is this person's when its actor, the name they presented, `detail.sub` or `detail.presented` is their name, a name they have been seen under, or their subject, exactly as spelled. Each row is an audit event. `authenticationEventsMatched` is how many, `authenticationEventsPaging` pages them under `authenticationsPage`, `authenticationEventsKnownActors` says which actors have a user page, and `authenticationEventsLog` says how full the audit log is. The member is not `authentications`: that one is the count. The list holds no more history than the audit log does, and nothing in it is a credential. Another process's rows are read before this reply is built when `user` is set, the same wait GET /admin-api/audit makes.\n\nTHE LIST IS CAPPED AND THE COUNT IS NOT (#610). `directoryPeople` is how many people the realm's directory defines, counted by the directory without listing them (null where it offers no count). The rows are at most `scanLimit` of those people — `scanned` of them read, `capped` true when the directory held more — united with everybody this service has seen, so `known` is the length of that list and never the size of the directory. `directoryCounts` is what the page's tiles draw, over every person the directory defines, without listing them: `people`; `signedInHere`, those of them the register has seen authenticate (it keeps the most recent identities only), and `neverSignedInHere`, the rest; `withSecond`, `withTotp`, `withKeys`, `primaryKeys`, `unreadable`, `withPassword`, `passwordOnly` (a password and no second factor) and `noWayIn` (neither a password nor a sign-in key). Null where the directory offers no such count. `factors`, `authenticatedHere` and the `factor` filter stay the LIST's. A client comparing this resource with SCIM's `totalResults` reads `directoryPeople`, never `known` (#684).\n\nTHE COUNT HAS AN AGE (#684). `directoryCountedAt` is when this process's directory counted `directoryCounts` (ISO 8601), and `directoryCountsKeptFor` the most seconds it keeps that count against another process's committed writes — its own writes, and the ones it has applied, end it at once. Two requests answered by two workers inside that window may carry two figures. Both are null where the directory offers no count, and `directoryCountsKeptFor` is null where no other process can lag it (the directory in memory).\n\n**Mirrors** `GET /admin/users` on the admin console.",
    `tags`:#["Users"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"The list, or one identity.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `400`:#{
        `description`:"The answer.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `default`:#{
        `description`:"A refusal or a failure.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      }
    },
    `parameters`:#[
      #{
        `name`:"user",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"One identity, as the list's `key` names them. Returns the drill-down instead of the list."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"q",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Substring of the identity key, case-insensitive."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"protocol",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Only identities that authenticated through this protocol family. The list's `protocols` member says which values there are; it is read off the data, so a family nobody has used is not offered."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"factor",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Only people holding a second factor of this kind, as on GET /admin-api/mfa."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"kind",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"`service`: only service accounts (person entries carrying `stsServiceAccount`); `person`: everybody else. Each row says which in `serviceAccount`, and `serviceAccounts` counts them over the whole list."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"authenticationsPage",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Which page of `authenticationEvents`, on the `?user=` reply only. Named rather than the bare `page` so it does not move the session list. The member is not `authentications`: that one is the count."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"page",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Which page of the match to return. CLAMPED rather than refused: a page past the end returns the last one, and the reply says which page it actually is."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"per",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Rows per page. Defaults to 50 and is capped at 300. On a drill-down it is SHARED by every list in the reply, which each carry a page number of their own."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"sessionsPage",
