@endpoint("/admin-api/users/clear-backup-codes",
  "post",
  #{
    `operationId`:"clearUserBackupCodes",
    `summary`:"Clear somebody's recovery codes",
    `description`:"**THE ONLY ROUTE TO A SECOND SET.** A set is issued automatically, and ONCE, the first time somebody asks for one from the user portal; does nothing while a set exists, so clearing is what lets the next enrolment issue one.\n\n**It cannot lock anybody out** — a recovery code is never a way in on its own — but it removes the way BACK, so somebody whose set is cleared and who then loses their phone needs an operator again.\n\n**There is no operation that READS the codes and there will not be.** They are a working second factor; `GET /users` reports how many remain and never what they are. The person reads their own set back on `/portal/mfa`, which is the only place in this service that shows one.\n\nClearing a set nobody holds answers 400 rather than 200, for `clear-totp`'s reason.\n\n**Mirrors** `POST /admin/users, POST /admin/users/new, POST /admin/users/edit and POST /admin/users/service-account` on the admin console.",
    `tags`:#["Users"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"Whose set was cleared. `sessionsEnded` counts the sessions a recovery code signed in, which were ended (#722); every other stays.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `400`:#{
        `description`:"The operation was refused; `errors` says why and nothing changed.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `default`:#{
        `description`:"A refusal or a failure.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      }
    },
    `requestBody`:#{`required`:true,`content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}},
    `security`:#[#{`oauth2`:#["admin:write"]},#{`bearerAuth`:#[]}]
  })
