@endpoint("/admin-api/rbac",
  "get",
  #{
    `operationId`:"getAdminRoles",
    `summary`:"Who may use the admin console, and how the gate is set",
    `description`:"The two roles — **Admin Read** and **Admin Write** — with every grant, the settings behind the gate, and who the CALLER is (`you`).\n\nThe roles are two ORDINARY GROUPS in the embedded LDAP directory (`admin.readGroup`, `admin.writeGroup`), not a store of the console's own. So this resource, `/admin/rbac`, an `ldapmodify` on 389 or 636 and a SCIM PATCH of the group are four doors onto one membership, and a grant made through any of them is visible through all of them.\n\n**WRITE IMPLIES READ.** A member of the write group does not also need the read group.\n\n**The bootstrap administrator** (`admin.bootstrapUsername`, reported in `bootstrap`) is made a member of both groups at startup. In DEVELOPMENT mode, until it first signs in to `/admin` (`bootstrap.claimedAt`), `openToAnyone` is true and anybody who signs in holds both roles; a process that never seeded it (`bootstrap.seeded` false) keeps the older rule: open while NEITHER group has a member. **In PRODUCT mode the window never opens** (`windowOpens` false, `openToAnyone` false whatever `admin.openWhenEmpty` says), and until the bootstrap administrator has claimed the console (`bootstrapPasswordRequired` true) its roles are honoured only from a PASSWORD sign-in through its own realm, which is also the only sign-in that claims it — a federation partner, a certificate, a wallet or a Kerberos ticket naming it holds nothing. `admin.openWhenEmpty` turns the open window off, and `closedToEveryone` reports a console no browser can reach, which is what this resource is the way out of.\n\n`enforced` reports whether the roster decides anything. It is always true now — the console gate became unconditional on 2026-09-06 — and the field is kept because a client reading it should not have to know that.\n\n**EVERY LIST IN THE REPLY IS PAGED.** `grants` by `page` and `per`, with `page`, `pages`, `perPage`, `firstRow`, `lastRow` and `matched` at the top level; `candidates` by `candidatesPage` and the same `per`, answered in `candidatesPaging`. `roles` carries each role's counts (`memberCount`, `presentCount`, `danglingCount`, `claimedCount`) and, since 2026-09-13, not its `members` and `claimed` lists: those were every membership unpaged, and they are the rows `grants` pages — `?role=read` narrows it to one role.\n\n**Mirrors** `GET /admin/rbac` on the admin console.",
    `tags`:#["Admin roles"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"The roster, the settings, and who is asking.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `400`:#{
        `description`:"The answer.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `default`:#{
        `description`:"A refusal or a failure.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      }
    },
    `parameters`:#[
      #{
        `name`:"q",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Substring of the person's name or of the raw membership value, case-insensitive."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"role",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Only grants of this role."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"personq",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Narrows `candidates` — the people a role could be granted to — to usernames containing this, case-insensitive. Where the directory is held in memory the list is the directory and who has signed in; where it is a window (#359) it is the direct children of ou=users, a page at a time, and a person who has signed in with no entry is not listed (a typed name can still be granted). `candidateSearch` says how many candidates there are in all and how many matched. A windowed search's count stops at `countCap` (100,000, the furthest the pager reaches; #1308): where more match, `matched` is `countCap` and `countCapped` is true — MORE THAN `matched` match, and `candidatesPaging` stops there. Held in memory, the count is exact and `countCapped` is false. `complete` says whether this page holds every one it counted: where it is false, `incomplete` says how many names a DN cannot hold were left out (`unusable`), how many counted rows the page did not reach (`short`, most often two entries sharing one RDN value), and whether the list stops at the walk's cap (`capped`, `cap`)."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"candidatesPage",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Which page of the matching `candidates`, answered in `candidatesPaging`. Clamped like `page`. The page size is `per` when given — shared with `grants`, as on every reply here carrying two lists — and otherwise twenty, the size of the console's results pane. `candidates` was the whole list until 2026-09-13, which on a directory of thousands was thousands of rows on every read of the roster."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"personfrom",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"The console results pane's OFFSET into the matching candidates, answered as the page it falls on. Ignored when `candidatesPage` is given, and when it is past the end."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"person",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"A username to look up among ALL the candidates, not only the page shown. `picked.candidate` is its row, or null when nobody by that name could be picked."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"page",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Which page of the match to return. CLAMPED rather than refused: a page past the end returns the last one, and the reply says which page it actually is."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"per",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Rows per page. Defaults to 50 and is capped at 300. On a drill-down it is SHARED by every list in the reply, which each carry a page number of their own."
      }
    ],
    `security`:#[#{`oauth2`:#["admin:read"]},#{`bearerAuth`:#[]}]
  })
