@endpoint("/admin-api/mfa",
  "get",
  #{
    `operationId`:"getMultiFactor",
    `summary`:"Who holds a second factor, and how RFC 6238 is configured here",
    `description`:"One row per person — this realm's directory people and everybody this service has SEEN, unioned — saying whether they hold a password, how many of their passkeys may sign them in on their own (`primaryKeys`) and may be a SECOND factor (`mfaKeys`), and whether an authenticator app is enrolled. A passkey has no role since #1135: both counts are of the same keys, by what the realm lets a key do, so each is every key the person holds or none.\n\n**TWO MECHANISMS COUNT AS A SECOND FACTOR HERE**: any passkey, where the realm lets one be a second factor, and an RFC 6238 authenticator app. A person holding either is asked for it at the sign-in screen and a password alone will not sign them in; `secondFactor` says which one they will be asked for, and somebody holding both is asked for the key with the code offered as the alternative.\n\n**`totpUsable: false` IS THE ROW TO LOOK FOR.** It means an enrolment exists that this process cannot read — almost always a shared secret sealed under a key-encryption key that has since been rotated. Those people are REFUSED at the code step rather than let through on one factor, so they cannot sign in at all until the enrolment is cleared.\n\n**CODES ARE VERIFIED FOR REAL IN BOTH MODES**, which almost nothing else in this service is. `totp` carries the algorithm table, read from the module that performs the algorithm.\n\nThe scan stops at 5,000 people (`capped`), because reading a credential per person happens on the one thread that answers every socket this service holds — the scan, not the count: `counts` is over the capped roster, and `directoryPeople` and `directoryCounts` over every person the directory defines, as on GET /admin-api/users (#610).\n\n**Mirrors** `GET /admin/users` on the admin console.",
    `tags`:#["Multi-factor authentication"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"The roster, the counts, and the RFC 6238 settings.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `400`:#{
        `description`:"The answer.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `default`:#{
        `description`:"A refusal or a failure.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      }
    },
    `parameters`:#[
      #{
        `name`:"q",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Substring of the person's name, case-insensitive."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"factor",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"`any` is anybody holding a second factor of either kind; `none` is the complement of it; `unreadable` is a factor whose sealed secret this service can no longer open."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"page",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Which page of the match to return. CLAMPED rather than refused: a page past the end returns the last one, and the reply says which page it actually is."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"per",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Rows per page. Defaults to 50 and is capped at 300. On a drill-down it is SHARED by every list in the reply, which each carry a page number of their own."
      }
    ],
    `security`:#[#{`oauth2`:#["admin:read"]},#{`bearerAuth`:#[]}]
  })
