@endpoint("/admin-api/oauth2",
  "get",
  #{
    `operationId`:"getOauth2Settings",
    `summary`:"The authorization server's own settings",
    `description`:"The `oauth2.*` settings: the issuer identifier, RFC 9700 mode, OAuth 2.1 mode (`oauth2.oauth21`, draft-ietf-oauth-v2-1-16, which implies RFC 9700 mode), the registered redirect URIs and the loopback port wildcard, Front-Channel Logout, the refresh token idle timeout, whether a sign-out revokes refresh tokens, the client assertion clock skew, the four lifetimes `GET /token-lifetimes` also reports — and `oauth2.breakIdTokenNonce`, which makes this service return an ID Token whose `nonce` is WRONG so that a client can be shown to check it — in development mode only; a product realm ignores it and refuses setting it.\n\n`oauth2.rfc9700` is restart-only and says so in `restartReason`: `global.https` derives from it and a listener's scheme is settled when the socket is bound. A TRUST REALM can carry it while the process does not, which is how one process answers permissively at /oauth2/authorize and enforces the BCP under a realm prefix.\n\nTHERE IS NO POST BESIDE THIS ONE and that is not a gap: every form on the console page this mirrors posts `set-many` to /admin/config, so `POST /admin-api/config/set-many` is already the operation for it. One store, one action, two doors.\n\n**Mirrors** `GET /admin/oauth2` on the admin console.\n\nThe reply also carries `protocolEndpoints`: every endpoint of this family in the realm the call arrived in, as `{ name, methods, url }` (plus `route` for an HTTP endpoint, `transport` for a socket, and `registered: false` for a route the router no longer has).",
    `tags`:#["OAuth 2.0 / OIDC"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"What the page says, and the settings it draws — described rows carrying each value's source and whether it can be changed while the service runs.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `default`:#{
        `description`:"A refusal or a failure.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      }
    },
    `security`:#[#{`oauth2`:#["admin:read"]},#{`bearerAuth`:#[]}]
  })
op `getOauth2Settings`(`responses/200/content/application~1json/schema`: `SharedModel186`,
  `responses/default/content/application~1json/schema`: `SharedModel1`): void;
  

@endpoint("/admin-api/oid4vci-settings",
  "get",
  #{
    `operationId`:"getOid4vciSettings",
    `summary`:"The credential issuer's own settings",
    `description`:"The `oid4vci.*` settings, among them the wallet an offer sends a holder to, the authorization server the credential endpoint will take a token from, the batch size, the deferred issuance timings, the offer username, whether a credential request must be encrypted, and the two that decide whether the SD-JWT VC and `ldp_vc` issuers name themselves by `did:web` or by URL.\n\nThose two are restart-only and they change what a VERIFIER has to resolve — a key fetched from a DID document rather than from JWKS. What a credential CONTAINS is `GET /credential-claims`.\n\nTHERE IS NO POST BESIDE THIS ONE and that is not a gap: every form on the console page this mirrors posts `set-many` to /admin/config, so `POST /admin-api/config/set-many` is already the operation for it. One store, one action, two doors.\n\n**Mirrors** `GET /admin/vc` on the admin console.\n\nThe reply also carries `protocolEndpoints`: every endpoint of this family in the realm the call arrived in, as `{ name, methods, url }` (plus `route` for an HTTP endpoint, `transport` for a socket, and `registered: false` for a route the router no longer has).",
