@endpoint("/admin-api/saml-idp-initiated",
  "get",
  #{
    `operationId`:"getSamlIdpInitiatedApplications",
    `summary`:"The SAML applications a sign-in may be started here for, with each one's link",
    `description`:"Every SAML 2.0 service provider and SAML 1.1 relying party in this realm for which an identity-provider-initiated sign-in would be ALLOWED: the realm's `saml2.idpInitiatedSso` / `saml11.idpInitiatedSso` (off by default), unless the entry's `saml2IdpInitiatedSso` / `saml11IdpInitiatedSso` says otherwise — asked through the function `GET /launch/{app}` asks, so a row here is never one the link refuses with STS-LAUNCH-0005. One row per application and family; an application on in both is two rows.\n\n`launchUrl` is the link a person follows (`/launch/{app}?protocol=`, this realm's, on the sign-in service's listener) and `endpoint` the protocol's own unsolicited endpoint the link hands the browser to. A row whose `missing` is not empty is switched on and cannot work yet — it has no registered assertion consumer service, and the link answers STS-LAUNCH-0006 — and is listed so that it can be fixed.\n\n`realmSwitches` says whether each realm setting is on: with both off, only an entry's own override can put a row here.\n\nPaged on `idpInitiatedPage` and `per`, by identifier.\n\n**Mirrors** `GET /admin/saml2` on the admin console.\n\nThe reply also carries `protocolEndpoints`: every endpoint of this family in the realm the call arrived in, as `{ name, methods, url }` (plus `route` for an HTTP endpoint, `transport` for a socket, and `registered: false` for a route the router no longer has).",
    `tags`:#["SAML 2.0"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"One page of the applications, its paging and the realm's two switches.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
