@endpoint("/admin-api/applications/fga-enable",
  "post",
  #{
    `operationId`:"enableApplicationFga",
    `summary`:"Enforce an application's fine-grained authorization policy",
    `description`:"Sets `appFgaEnforced`: from the next request every RFC 9396 authorization detail of a type this application declares is decided by its own policy, at the authorization, PAR and token endpoints, and so is every AuthZEN evaluation whose resource resolves to it. The policy is created from `fga-default` first if it has none. Refused while a declared type is not covered (`STS-FGA-0018`, naming each type and its uncovered member paths, `*` for an open schema), while its policy is disabled (`STS-FGA-0006`) or does not parse (`STS-FGA-0007`), and for an application this realm does not hold (`STS-FGA-0009`). `failure` names which. Every enable, including one of an application that already enforces, also grants each holder of REMOTE_PEPS in the realm (the members of `roles.remotePepGroup`) membership of `roles.authzenPepGroup` and the application in its AuthZEN scope, where either is missing, so a remote PEP's edge PDP may forward evaluations about it. A holder that cannot be granted is listed with why (`STS-FGA-0025`) and does not fail the enable. Disabling removes nothing.\n\n**Mirrors** `POST /admin/applications, POST /admin/applications/new, POST /admin/applications/edit and POST /admin/applications/owners` on the admin console.",
    `tags`:#["Applications"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"The `policy` it is decided by, `enforced: true`, and `peps`: the remote PEPs `granted` (with `member` and `scope` saying what was added), those that `held` both already, and those `failed`, each with `why` and `code`.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
