@endpoint("/admin-api/kerberos/principals",
  "get",
  #{
    `operationId`:"getKerberosPrincipals",
    `summary`:"Who the KDC holds a stored long-term key for",
    `description`:"Three lists, paged separately with `?peoplePage=`, `?servicesPage=` and `?databasePage=` and one `?per=`.\n\n`people` — the directory people whose Kerberos keys were derived from their own password when it was set or verified (PRODUCT MODE): the principal, the kvno, the enctypes, when and on what event the keys were derived, whether they are sealed, and `current` — whether they still match the password the entry holds. A person whose keys are not current is refused by the KDC until their next verified sign-in derives new ones.\n\n`services` — the service principals created with a RANDOM key: the principal, the kvno, the enctypes, when created and last rotated.\n\n`krbtgt` (#169) — the realm's krbtgt key: where it comes from (`stored`, `password` in development, `none`, `unreadable`), its kvno and enctypes, when it was made and last rotated, the versions kept and until when, and the schedule — `scheduled`, `offReason`, `intervalDays`, `nextDueAt`.\n\n`database` (#595) — every principal the KDC holds, the development fixtures and the names created on first sight among them: the principal, its name type, salt, enctypes and kvno, and its flags, with `fixtureAccounts` and `realmsServed` beside it. What the demonstration document `GET /krb5/principals` listed until #595 deleted it, without its two development passwords.\n\n**NO KEY MATERIAL IS IN THIS REPLY**, sealed or otherwise — both lists are built from the public info attributes. A trust realm has a KDC and a principal database of its own since 2026-09-15, so a realm prefix answers that realm's principals.\n\n**Mirrors** `GET /admin/kerberos` on the admin console.\n\nThe reply also carries `protocolEndpoints`: every endpoint of this family in the realm the call arrived in, as `{ name, methods, url }` (plus `route` for an HTTP endpoint, `transport` for a socket, and `registered: false` for a route the router no longer has).",
    `tags`:#["Kerberos"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"The three lists with their paging, whether this is a product KDC, whether krb5.personKeys is on, the enctypes and starting kvno, and the acceptor's SPN.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      },
      `default`:#{
        `description`:"A refusal or a failure.",
        `content`:#{`application/json`:#{`schema`:null}}
      }
    },
    `parameters`:#[
      #{
        `name`:"per",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Rows per page. Defaults to 50 and is capped at 300. On a drill-down it is SHARED by every list in the reply, which each carry a page number of their own."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"peoplePage",
        `in`:"query",
        `required`:false,
        `schema`:null,
        `description`:"Which page of `people` to return, answered by `peoplePaging`. Directory people holding keys derived from their own password."
      },
      #{
        `name`:"servicesPage",
