    `operationId`:"getKerberosTrusts",
    `summary`:"The realm's Kerberos trusts, with their keys described and never carried",
    `description`:"The trust records of the realm the call is in, paged with `?trustsPage=` and `?per=`, and one record whole with `?trust=<partner realm>` (`trust`, or `unknownTrust` naming a partner this realm holds no trust with).\n\nEach row: the partner realm, NetBIOS name and domain SID, the type (`external`, `trust-realm`), the direction, the [MS-ADTS] 6.1.6.7.9 attributes by name and as the `trustAttributes` mask, the [MS-PAC] 4.1.2.2 boundary, the routed DNS suffixes, the enctypes, the forest trust information and claims transformation, and per key (`incoming`, `outgoing`) whether it is held, its kvno, its source and when it was set — the incoming side with the PREVIOUS version a rotation keeps and until when it is accepted. `health` lists the findings the console's health column draws (`kind`, `level`, `text`).\n\n`form` is what the add and edit forms are drawn from: the closed attribute set with its contradictions and requirements, the types, directions, key sources, enctypes, claims transformations, the shortest trust password, and the other trust realms a `trust-realm` partner may name. `settings` are the four the trusts read. **NO KEY MATERIAL IS IN THIS REPLY.**\n\n**Mirrors** `GET /admin/kerberos` on the admin console.\n\nThe reply also carries `protocolEndpoints`: every endpoint of this family in the realm the call arrived in, as `{ name, methods, url }` (plus `route` for an HTTP endpoint, `transport` for a socket, and `registered: false` for a route the router no longer has).",
    `tags`:#["Kerberos"],
    `responses`:#{
      `200`:#{
        `description`:"The trust records with their paging and health, the drill-down record, what the forms are drawn from, and the trust settings.",
