  }

@js(#{
    `description`:"ON BY DEFAULT. On, the sign-in screen offers *Sign in with a passkey* and the username field's autofill, which ask the browser for any discoverable credential of this realm (WebAuthn Level 3 section 5.4, `allowCredentials` empty) and sign in the account its user handle names, with user verification required and checked. Off, neither is offered and a sign-in always names the person first. Replaces the setting webauthn.usernameless. (`true`/`false`, or `TRUE`/`FALSE` from a form.) Default true.",
    `$comb`:"oneOf"
  }) @hoisted union post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_allowUsernameless_h3029 {boolean, string}

@js(#{
    `description`:"OFF BY DEFAULT. On, an application whose authentication mechanism is a passkey and nothing else (the authentication policy it names, appAuthnPolicy, lists `webauthn` alone) gets a sign-in screen with no username field: *Sign in with a passkey* is the way in, and a sign-in that names a person is refused. It needs the row above. The realm's `default` profile does not apply it to the admin console, the debugger or the user portal; a named profile selecting that application does. (`true`/`false`, or `TRUE`/`FALSE` from a form.) Default false.",
    `$comb`:"oneOf"
  }) @hoisted union post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_requireUsernameless_h3030 {boolean, string}

@js(#{
    `description`:"OFF BY DEFAULT. On, registration asks the authenticator for its minimum PIN length (CTAP 2.1 section 12.4, the minPinLength extension), records it on the key, and refuses a key whose minimum is below the next row, at registration and at every sign-in. A key answers only for relying parties it was configured to tell (CTAP 2.1 setMinPINLength with this RP ID); every other key, and every platform or synced passkey, does not report, and is refused unless the row after next is on. (`true`/`false`, or `TRUE`/`FALSE` from a form.) Default false.",
    `$comb`:"oneOf"
  }) @hoisted union post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_enforcePinLength_h3031 {boolean, string}

@js(#{`minimum`:4,`maximum`:63}) @hoisted scalar post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_minPinLength_v0_h3032 extends integer;
  

@js(#{
    `description`:"The shortest PIN a security key may be set to accept, as the key itself reports it, while the row above is on. Between 4 (CTAP 2.1's own floor) and 63. Between 4 and 63. Default 4.",
    `$comb`:"oneOf"
  }) @hoisted union post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_minPinLength_h3033 {
    post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_minPinLength_v0_h3032,
    string
  }

@js(#{
    `description`:"OFF BY DEFAULT, which is the strict reading: while the PIN length is enforced, a key that does not report it is refused. On, such a key is accepted and only a reported minimum is held to the rule — so the rule then binds only keys configured to report to this relying party. (`true`/`false`, or `TRUE`/`FALSE` from a form.) Default false.",
    `$comb`:"oneOf"
  }) @hoisted union post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_pinLengthOnlyIfSupported_h3034 {boolean, string}

@js(#{
    `description`:"OFF BY DEFAULT. The attestation rules (the webauthn.attestation* settings: the policy, the AAGUID list, the certification level and FIPS) are checked when a passkey is registered. On, they are checked again at every sign-in against what was recorded then and the FIDO Metadata Service as it is now, so a key registered before a rule was tightened, or whose model was since reported compromised, stops signing anybody in. A key with no trusted attestation fails every rule that demands one. (`true`/`false`, or `TRUE`/`FALSE` from a form.) Default false.",
    `$comb`:"oneOf"
  }) @hoisted union post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_enforceAttestationAtSignIn_h3035 {boolean, string}

@js(#{
    `description`:"ON BY DEFAULT: one \"Use passkey\" step whose request lists every one of the person's passkeys, and the browser offers whichever is present. Off, a person with more than one is shown a choice per passkey, by its name, and the request names only that one. (`true`/`false`, or `TRUE`/`FALSE` from a form.) Default true.",
    `$comb`:"oneOf"
  }) @hoisted union post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_aggregateDevices_h3036 {boolean, string}

@js(#{
    `description`:"OFF BY DEFAULT. On, an issuance to an application this profile covers waits until the sign-on session is bound to the browser's key, and is refused when the browser skipped that registration or the wait elapsed without a binding. A session that was never offered registration is not refused by this row. Refused while authn.deviceBoundSessions is off. (`true`/`false`, or `TRUE`/`FALSE` from a form.) Default false.",
    `$comb`:"oneOf"
  }) @hoisted union post_admin_api_policies_save_passkey_policy_b_application_json_deviceBoundRequired_h3037 {boolean, string}

@endpoint("/admin-api/policies/save-passkey-policy",
  "post",
  #{
    `operationId`:"savePasskeyPolicy",
    `summary`:"Set the passkey policy profile",
    `description`:"Writes `cn=default,ou=passkeyPolicies` in this realm's directory, REPLACING what is there; a realm with no entry of its own follows the default realm's. `allowUsernameless` (on by default) offers a passkey sign-in with no username. `requireUsernameless` (off, #608) makes it the only way in for an application whose named authentication policy (`appAuthnPolicy`) lists `webauthn` alone as its first factor: no username field, and a sign-in naming a person refused; it needs `allowUsernameless`, and the `default` profile never applies it to the admin console, the debugger or the user portal. While it is off, \"Use a security key\" asks the authenticator for a discoverable credential (`residentKey: required`), as \"Create a passkey\" always does; while it is on, it asks `securityKeyResidentKey` (`discouraged`, `preferred` or `required`, default `required`). `backupEligibility` (`allow` by default) set to `disallow` refuses a synced (backup-eligible) passkey at registration and at sign-in. `enforcePinLength` (off) asks a key for its minimum PIN length (CTAP 2.1 minPinLength) and holds it to `minPinLength` (4 to 63); `pinLengthOnlyIfSupported` (off) accepts a key that does not report. `enforceAttestationAtSignIn` (off) holds every passkey sign-in to the attestation rules in force. `passkeyHints`, `securityKeyHints` and `signInHints` are the WebAuthn hints each ceremony sends, in order, or `none`. `enterpriseSerialAttribute` (empty) names the attribute of a person's entry holding their security-key serials, which a registration's trusted enterprise attestation must name. `userDisplayName`, `rpNameExtras` and `credentialLabel` are the names a passkey prompt and a new key show. `aggregateDevices` (on) offers a person's passkeys as one sign-in choice; off, one per passkey. `postQuantumRequired` (`off`, #718) set to `sign-in` lets only a post-quantum (ML-DSA) passkey sign a person in, as the first credential or the second factor, and `registration-and-sign-in` refuses a classical one at registration too; refused while the realm offers no ML-DSA (STS-AUTHN-0357). `attestationAlgorithms` (`none`, #941, optional) lists the algorithms a passkey's ATTESTATION must be signed with, statement and every certificate leaf to anchor: `post-quantum`, `ml-dsa-44`, `ml-dsa-65`, `ml-dsa-87`, `slh-dsa`, `composite-ml-dsa`; set, it demands a trusted statement and refuses any other at registration (STS-AUTHN-0411), and at sign-in while `enforceAttestationAtSignIn` is on (STS-AUTHN-0412). `postQuantumAllowCredentials` (`realm`, #946; optional) chooses how the passkey step names the keys while one is required: `narrow` the post-quantum ones only, `list-and-refuse` every one, a classical assertion then refused after the ceremony with a page saying why, and `realm` the realm's webauthn.postQuantumAllowCredentials. `registrationAlgorithms` (`realm`, #940; optional) is the ordered list a registration offers this profile's people in place of the realm's webauthn.algorithms, and `registrationMode` (`realm`, #940; optional) is `pq-only` (ML-DSA only, no classical retry), `pq-first` (ML-DSA, then the classical algorithms, whatever the realm's pqcOnly), `classical-only` (no ML-DSA, no post-quantum lane) or `realm` (webauthn.pqcOnly and webauthn.pqcAlongsideClassical); a passkey outside what the profile offers is refused at registration (STS-AUTHN-0432). `clientRules` (empty, #1112; optional) names the clients — `<browser> on <system>`, each a family or `*` with an optional major-version range, `;` between rules — whose registrations offer the list less ML-DSA, where a classical passkey is allowed. Refused: `classical-only` with `postQuantumRequired` not `off` or with `attestationAlgorithms` set (STS-AUTHN-0430), and `pq-only` or `pq-first` where the list offered names no ML-DSA (STS-AUTHN-0431).\n\n**Every field is required** and one left out is refused by name rather than reset to a default, because a save that quietly loosened a rule nobody mentioned is the mistake nobody sees.\n\n**Mirrors** `POST /admin/policies` on the admin console.",
